{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794808040435,"sessionID":"ses_e1d1c612bffenqRaMYnBfMfTGv","part":{"partID":"prt_1e2e54b45001P3HmT6UIMV6XLR","sessionID":"ses_e1d1c612bffenqRaMYnBfMfTGv","messageID":"msg_1e2e53f1f001UBXfYHcr7ApJJ1","type":"tool","id":"call_01_8wpmbmk7xs0ix7vk2ac31vqz","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md, lines 1-190\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Session Handover Hook: Context-Limit Handoff and Fresh-Session Handover\"\n3: description: \"Shared core, per-runtime adapters, CLI and skilled-loop wrapper that hand unfinished work to a fresh session in the same terminal when a runtime nears its context limit.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"session handover hook\"\n6:   - \"skilled-loop relaunch\"\n7:   - \"context limit handoff\"\n8: ---\n9: # Session Handover\n10: \n11: > Shared adapters and the `skilled-loop` wrapper carry unfinished work into a fresh runtime session.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: The `session-handover/` folder holds the shared handoff logic, one adapter per automatic runtime, the CLI and the tests for the session handover loop. It also holds the history lookup: `bin/history-lookup.mjs`, the runnable entry for the printed lookup command, and `history/`, the engine that reads the sender's earlier turns back. Section 3 explains how the lookup works end to end. Each adapter asks for a handoff near its context threshold. The sender prepares a note and the adapter arms a matching record. After the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` relaunches it with a claim seed when the record is armed, fresh and below the hop cap. If a record is still pending at exit, the wrapper prints a manual claim command for the selected runtime and consumes the record. `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE` builds that command from the first launch's name and Pi extension flags, and from the model, provider and effort the record carries when it has them, so after a hop that switched model the printed command names the model the session was last using. The killer uses the same template in its timeout notification.\n18: \n19: Claude Code uses `PreToolUse` for Bash, `PostToolUse`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop` and `SessionStart`, both after a compaction and at startup for the claim of a hop whose start prompt is the goal command. Pi, OpenCode, Codex and Vibe use their host events or a plugin. Cursor and Devin ship on the command trigger only. Start every runtime through `skilled-loop` for the loop, by hand or through the shell shim in section 2. Inside the loop the nudge asks for a hop. Outside it, the nudge asks only for a note the session keeps through auto-compaction, as section 3 describes under Outside The Loop.\n20: \n21: ### Runtime adapter matrix\n22: \n23: | Runtime | Trigger | Relaunch seed syntax | Model carry | Mirror form | Spike evidence | Verdict |\n24: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n25: | Claude | automatic | `claude [--model <model>] \"<seed>\" [--name <name>]` | `--model`, `--model=` | [.claude/settings.json](../../../.claude/settings.json) hook entries | [SPIKE-REPORT.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/001-claude-relaunch-loop/scratch/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md) | AUTO |\n26: | Pi | automatic | `pi [--provider <p>] [--model <model>] [-ne] [-e <path>] [--name <name>] \"<seed>\"` | `--model`, `--provider`, extension flags | `.pi/extensions/` one-line entry file that re-exports `pi/session-handover.ts` | [SPIKE-REPORT.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/002-pi-opencode-adapters/scratch/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md) | AUTO |\n27: | OpenCode | automatic | `opencode [-m <model>] --prompt \"<seed>\"`, and on OpenCode 2 `opencode --standalone --prompt \"<seed>\"` | `-m`, `--model`, `--model=` on OpenCode 1 only, because the OpenCode 2 root command has no model flag | `.opencode/plugins/` plugin plus `opencode/` browsability symlink | [SPIKE-REPORT.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/002-pi-opencode-adapters/scratch/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md) | AUTO |\n28: | Codex | automatic | `codex --no-daemon [-m <model>] \"<seed>\"` | `-m`, `--model`, `--model=` | [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json) hook entries plus `.codex/hooks/` symlinks | [SPIKE-codex.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/003-command-trigger-runtimes/scratch/spike/SPIKE-codex.md) | AUTO |\n29: | Cursor | `/speckit-handoff` | `cursor-agent [--model <model>] \"<seed>\"` | `--model`, `--model=` | [.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md](../../../.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md) symlink | Cursor spike, packet `023-session-relaunch-loop/003` | COMMAND-ONLY |\n30: | Devin | `/speckit:handoff` | `devin [--model <model>] -- \"<seed>\"` | `--model`, `--model=` | none, [.devin/SYNC.md](../../../.devin/SYNC.md) carries no command surface | [SPIKE-devin.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/003-command-trigger-runtimes/scratch/spike/SPIKE-devin.md) | COMMAND-ONLY |\n31: | Vibe | automatic from a context nudge, and under `skilled-loop` a typed `/speckit-handoff` arms too, because the session reads its id from the `session-id` command; outside the loop it uses a `uuidgen` id that is never armed, so that route prints the claim command | `vibe \"<seed>\"` | none, because `--model` is headless-only | [.vibe/hooks.toml](../../../.vibe/hooks.toml) entries for `turn-start`, `turn-end` and `compaction-post-tool`, plus [.vibe/prompts/speckit-handoff.md](../../../.vibe/prompts/speckit-handoff.md) for the manual start | [resume-probe.md](../../../specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/019-vibe-relaunch-command-tier/scratch/resume-probe.md) | AUTO |\n32: \n33: `skilled-loop` exports `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` with the selected runtime. It carries the first launch's model for every runtime. Pi also carries its provider. Claude and Pi carry their name. Pi carries its extension flags. Relative Pi extension paths resolve against the wrapper's starting directory. `prepare` and `claim` require `--session <id>`. Claude can omit it and use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. Vibe under `skilled-loop` can omit it too: Vibe gives its tools no session id, so the turn-start hook records the id it sees for the loop, and `session-id --runtime vibe` prints it. Other runtimes pass the id directly. Runtimes with no session-id variable, such as Devin, Cursor or Vibe outside the loop, run `uuidgen` once and reuse the value. `CLAUDE_PID` supplies Claude's process id for the shell-child check, and Stop keeps it as the killer target while it descends from the loop. `complete` requires only the note path.\n34: \n35: ### Model And Effort Across A Hop\n36: \n37: A relaunched session starts on the model and effort the sender was using, where the runtime can be told them at launch. The wrapper replays the first launch's model and effort flags on every relaunch, in each runtime's own syntax. At `prepare` the CLI also reads the sender's latest values from its own session log on Claude and Codex and from the CLI config on Cursor, and the Pi extension adds them when it arms. All of them keep the values on the relaunch record, never in the note. `consume` hands them to the wrapper, and a value read from the log beats the launch flag for that hop. The value that was used then carries to later hops that record none.\n38: \n39: Model and effort move as one set, because an effort is chosen for one model and another model may reject it. When the hop's model differs from the launch model and the record carries no usable effort, the wrapper drops the launch effort and the receiver starts on the runtime's default effort. An arm that records a new model without an effort removes the effort the record held. A launch with no model flag has no model to compare, so its effort stands, and a live model equal to the launch model keeps the launch effort too.\n40: \n41: - **Claude:** model from `--model`, effort from `--effort` (`low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`). The session log's last main-chain assistant entry supplies `requestedModel`, or the response model, and `effort`. A `/model` or `/effort` switch is written to the log as soon as it is confirmed, as a local command entry and its stdout, and a switch confirmed after that assistant entry wins for its field, so it reaches the relaunch without waiting for another turn. `/effort` takes the level from its `Set effort level to <level>` line. `/model` takes the id typed after it; the picker leaves that empty and confirms with a display name such as `Opus 5.5`, which proves a switch but not an id, so the model reads as unproven and the launch model stands. A new model drops the old turn's effort, while an `/effort` switch of its own still counts. A cancelled command changes nothing.\n42: - **Codex:** model from `-m`, effort from `-c model_reasoning_effort=<level>` or `--config`, replayed as `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"<level>\"`. The rollout's last `turn_context` supplies `model` and `effort`.\n43: - **Pi:** model and provider as before, effort from `--thinking` (`off` up to `max`). A `:<thinking>` suffix on the model travels inside the model. The extension hands the session's current model, provider and thinking level to the record when it arms it. The provider is used only together with a live model the wrapper accepted. A launch `--model openai/gpt-6-luna` and a recorded `openai` plus `gpt-6-luna` are the same model, so that hop keeps the launch thinking level when the record has none. A recorded provider that differs from the launch provider, named by `--provider X` or as `X/model`, counts as a model switch, so that hop drops the launch thinking level when the record has none. A launch that names no provider has nothing to compare, so a provider change on the same model keeps the launch thinking level. When the plan-build extension is loaded, the sender's Plan or Build mode is replayed too: the hop starts with `--plan` or `--build` for the mode the sender was last in, in place of the launch flag.\n44: - **Cursor:** the model, with any bracketed `effort=` inside it. At `prepare` the CLI reads the current `model` from `~/.cursor/cli-config.json`. When `selectedModel` names the same model, its parameters are added as `<modelId>[<id>=<value>,...]`. Cursor rewrites `model` on every switch but `selectedModel` only for a parameterized choice, so a `selectedModel` naming another model is stale and is ignored. The file is shared by every Cursor session, so the CLI uses it only when it changed after the wrapper launched this hop, which the wrapper records as `SKILLED_LOOP_LAUNCHED_AT`. An older file may hold another session's choice, and the auto model has no id to pass, so in either case the launch flag stands.\n45: - **Devin:** the model only, because it has no effort flag. It reads no live value.\n46: - **OpenCode 2:** nothing, because its root command takes no model or effort flag. The receiver starts on the configured default.\n47: \n48: The Claude Stop adapter stamps a non-empty `payload.permission_mode` on the armed record as `permissionMode`, and `consume` prints it. A Claude hop and the manual claim command carry a usable live mode as `--permission-mode`. A launch `--permission-mode` is captured into `SKILLED_LOOP_PERMISSION_MODE`, so a hop with no usable live mode keeps the launch value. On a hop, a live `plan` mode keeps the launch mode and prints `note: the sender was in plan mode, the receiver starts in the launch mode` on stderr. A live `bypassPermissions` is replayed only when the launch line already carried `--dangerously-skip-permissions` or `--allow-dangerously-skip-permissions`, and the wrapper never adds an unlocking flag of its own. On a hop, any other live mode the wrapper cannot replay keeps the launch mode with a `warning:` line on stderr. Codex, Pi, OpenCode, Cursor and Devin record no permission mode, so a live permission mode never reaches their sessions.\n49: \n50: Claude and Pi carry the session name across a hop. On Claude, `prepare` records the last `custom-title` entry's `customTitle` from the session log as `name` ahead of `SKILLED_LOOP_NAME`, so a `/rename` reaches the receiver. A session with no rename and no launch name records the last `ai-title` entry's `aiTitle`, the title Claude generated, so the receiver keeps that title with a number and is not given a new one. Pi carries the name from its launch line. The wrapper strips the number it wrote for the sender, exports the stem and relaunches with `--name` set to the stem plus the next number, so the first receiver is `<stem> (2)` and every later receiver counts up without the suffixes stacking. On a hop, a record name that starts with `-` or holds a control character is ignored with a `warning:` line on stderr and the launch name stands. A manual claim prints the stem with no number.\n51: \n52: A level outside the runtime's list is dropped with a `warning:` line on stderr, and a logged model that does not look like a model id is ignored the same way, so a bad value never stops a relaunch.\n53: \n54: ### Automatic triggers on Codex\n55: \n56: Codex binds four repo hooks rendered from `hook-registry.json` into [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json). `prompt-seed.mjs` binds `UserPromptSubmit`: a `[session-handover]` prompt receives the claim-seed instructions, and a non-seed prompt first withdraws a record this session armed or a failed Stop deferred, as the Stop paragraph below describes, then runs the same threshold check as `context-nudge.mjs`, which binds `PostToolUse`. The Codex prompt payload carries only the prompt text, so any prompt other than a seed counts as the operator's. The Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only, so seed detection and the mid-session nudge are separate entry points over `shared.mjs`. The fill comes from the transcript rollout's latest `token_count` event: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` is the numerator and `cached_input_tokens` stays out because it is already inside that count. The window is `SESSION_HANDOVER_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, the envelope the repo's other Codex adapters emit. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only payloads whose `hook_event_name` is `Stop`. It resolves the target, arms a matching pending record, then spawns the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`. A spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer. A Stop that cannot arm, because it carried no transcript path, found no session process below the loop or the arm threw, stamps `deferredAt` on the pending record and prints one `systemMessage` that names the cause and says the next prompt withdraws the hand-over. `compact-pointer.mjs` binds `SessionStart` and acts only on `source: compact`. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the same ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses. `skilled-loop codex` always starts Codex with `--no-daemon` because its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment of the session... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n57: \n58: \n59: ### Command-only runtimes: Cursor and Devin\n60: \n61: Cursor ships on the command trigger for two reasons. Its spike evidence is source-only: the bundle's hook dispatch code was inspected, but no live payload was captured because the machine's Cursor credentials were expired and no permitted provider exists for Cursor. And `stop` is not wired in this repository's [.cursor/hooks.json](../../../.cursor/hooks.json), whose registered event list has no `stop`. Devin ships on the command trigger because its hook usage is unverified: the historical probe summarizes field names without a raw payload and current `SessionEnd` behavior is unknown, so an automatic arm cannot be proven.\n62: \n63: Start a handoff by running `/speckit:handoff` inside the session. It writes the note and runs `prepare`. Outside `skilled-loop`, prepare prints the manual claim command and exits 3, and that line starts the hop by hand. While the concern is off, prepare refuses in both places, as section 3 describes. Outside the loop a session runs this command only when the operator asks for a manual handoff. Otherwise it keeps working. The printed command uses the selected runtime's seed form: Claude, Pi, Codex and Cursor use the seed as a positional argument, OpenCode adds `--prompt`, and Devin adds `--` before the seed. `!` in a handoff path sits in single quotes outside the seed's double quotes. For example, the CLI and wrapper print `...handoff /tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md`, which interactive Bash preserves when history expansion is on. Cursor names a project command after its file, so in Cursor the command is `/speckit-handoff`, reached through [.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md](../../../.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md), a symlink to the canonical file. Devin has no command surface, so a Devin session reads [.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md](../../commands/speckit/handoff.md) directly and follows it. Under `skilled-loop`, a record prepared on a command-only runtime stays pending because no arm event exists for it, so the hop runs by launching the wrapper with the claim seed. On Cursor and Devin a successful prepare says the handoff is prepared and nothing ends this session, and tells the session to exit and run the claim command the wrapper prints.\n64: \n65: ### Runtime Behavior And Limits\n66: \n67: `SESSION_HANDOVER_THRESHOLD` controls when each session is nudged. Keep the threshold well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs at `0.12` chained hop after hop because each new session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches and stops the chain once that limit is reached.\n68: \n69: This repository's Pi setup needs `-ne -e` and the [.pi/extensions/session-handover.ts](../../../.pi/extensions/session-handover.ts) extension. Two installed Pi extensions both register `--fast`, so the default startup conflicts. The wrapper carries the selected extension flags across hops.\n70: \n71: In the recorded live runs, OpenCode reported about 1M context tokens for `openai/gpt-6-luna`. Pi reported 272K for the same model. The live handoff results and operator inputs are in the [Pi and OpenCode playbook](../../skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-handover-pi-opencode.md).\n72: \n73: ---\n74: \n75: ## 2. STARTING THE LOOP\n76: \n77: Start each runtime from the repository root through the wrapper:\n78: \n79: ~~~bash\n80: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop claude \"Work on the task and preserve unfinished work for the next session.\"\n81: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop pi --provider openai --model gpt-6-luna -ne -e .pi/extensions/session-handover.ts\n82: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop opencode -m openai/gpt-6-luna\n83: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop codex -m gpt-6-luna\n84: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop cursor\n85: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop devin\n86: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop vibe\n87: ~~~\n88: \n89: The wrapper accepts `skilled-loop [claude|pi|opencode|codex|cursor|devin|vibe] [args...]` and defaults to `claude`. Vibe's `--model` is headless-only, so a hop never replays it and the interactive relaunch keeps the configured `active_model`. The `cursor` arm launches the `cursor-agent` binary. A hop replays the first launch's options in their original order and replaces the start prompt with the claim seed. Model, effort and name come from the relaunch record when it carries a usable value for that field and from the launch line otherwise. The Pi provider comes from the record only together with a recorded model the wrapper accepted and from the launch provider otherwise. Pi extension settings come from the first launch's arguments. Flags that select or continue a session, including Claude `-c`, `--resume`, `--session-id` and `--cloud`, Pi `--session` and `--fork`, OpenCode `--session` and `--prompt`, Codex `--image`, `--worktree` and `--last`, Devin `--resume`, `--prompt-file` and `--print`, and Cursor `--worktree` and `--worktree-base`, are in the deny list and are not repeated. Options documented by each runtime's help are replayed with their values, including repeated options. If an unknown option is followed by a bare word, the wrapper warns and declines the hop because it cannot distinguish an option value from the start prompt. Relative Pi extension paths are resolved against the starting directory before a hop changes directories.\n90: \n91: The wrapper exports one loop id and starts `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` at 0. It relaunches only when a matching record is armed, fresh and below the configured hop limit.\n92: \n93: ### Run every session under the loop\n94: \n95: The session-handover hooks ship on for Claude Code and off for every other runtime. Set `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED=1` in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` or the environment to turn them on everywhere, or turn one runtime on or off with its own `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED` (section 5). Without an enable flag only Claude Code sessions are nudged, and auto-compaction stays the fallback on the others.\n96: \n97: Then add one line to `~/.zshrc` or `~/.bashrc`, with the absolute path of the checkout that owns `.skilled`:\n98: \n99: ~~~bash\n100: source /path/to/repo/.skilled/bin/skilled-loop-shell.sh\n101: ~~~\n102: \n103: The shim defines `claude`, `pi`, `opencode`, `codex`, `devin`, `cursor-agent` and `skilled-loop` as shell functions. The `skilled-loop` function runs the wrapper from the checkout that owns the sourced shim, so a pasted manual claim works from any directory and without the terminal check. Typing `claude` in a terminal then runs `bash <repo>/.skilled/bin/skilled-loop claude`, and `cursor-agent` runs the wrapper's `cursor` arm. A function hands the launch to the wrapper only when all of these hold:\n104: \n105: - `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` is unset, so no loop is running yet.\n106: - Standard input and standard output are both a terminal.\n107: - The arguments open an interactive session. Subcommands such as `claude doctor`, `codex exec` or `opencode run`, print modes such as `-p`, version and help run the binary directly. Each list comes from that runtime's own `--help`. Codex `resume` and `fork`, and Cursor `agent`, `ls` and `resume` stay wrapped, because they open a session.\n108: - A `.skilled/bin/skilled-loop` exists in the current directory or one of its parents. A `.skilled` that is a symlink counts, so the wrapper also starts in a repository that links this checkout's `.skilled`.\n109: \n110: The shim resolves only wrappers inside the sourced checkout, a linked worktree registered to it, or an absolute checkout listed in `SKILLED_LOOP_TRUSTED_CHECKOUTS` as a colon-separated value. An unset or empty variable trusts no extra checkout.\n111: \n112: The automatic hop in a linking repository also needs that repository's runtime folders, because each runtime reads its hooks or plugins from the project it starts in. Link `.claude`, `.codex`, `.opencode` and `.pi` from this checkout as well, or give each its own session-handover registration. A repository that links only `.skilled` starts under the wrapper but never receives the nudge, so its sessions hop only through `/speckit:handoff`.\n113: \n114: A wrapper runs the code it read when it started, so a change to `.skilled/bin/skilled-loop` reaches no hop of a loop that is already running. The wrapper takes a checksum of its own file before the first launch and compares it before each hop. When the file changed, it prints one line on stderr: `note: skilled-loop changed on disk after this loop started, restart the wrapper to run the new code`. It also appends a `wrapper-changed` line with the loop id to `relaunch.log`, so an unattended hop that scrolls the note away still leaves a record. A checksum that comes back empty, because a checkout replaced the file for a moment, is not a change and prints and logs nothing. The hop still goes ahead on the code the loop started with. Exit the session and start the runtime again to pick the change up.\n115: \n116: In every other case the function runs `command <binary> \"$@\"`. `skilled_loop_resolve <runtime> [args...]` prints the wrapper path the shim would use, or nothing. It skips the terminal check, so you can call it from a script to see the decision. Claude, Pi, OpenCode and Codex then hop on their own near the context threshold. Cursor and Devin run under the wrapper too, but they hop only after `/speckit:handoff`, as section 1 describes. An alias with the same name still takes precedence over the function, so remove a `claude` alias before you rely on the shim. Agent tool shells, such as Claude Code's, are rebuilt from a function snapshot that drops names starting with a single underscore, so every helper the shim's functions call keeps a plain `skilled_loop_` name.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 3. HANDOFF PROTOCOL\n121: \n122: Claude's `context-nudge` handles `PostToolUse` and `UserPromptSubmit`. It reads the latest main-chain assistant usage from the Claude transcript and compares the fill ratio with the configured threshold. The default threshold is 0.80. One more nudge fires 0.05 above that level. Each level fires once. A withdrawn or cancelled hand-over earns one extra nudge, as the Stop paragraph below describes. The adapter accepts session ids matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}`. Transcript read errors produce no usage. The reader starts with the last 256 KiB of the transcript. When that tail holds no assistant row, for example after a tool result larger than the tail, it widens the read up to 16 MiB before it reports no usage. The Codex reader widens the same way. Failed Claude settings reads are treated as absent.\n123: \n124: The nudge reports the fill as `[session-handover] Context is about N% full.`, or as `[session-handover] Context is past the configured window (over 100% full).` when the ratio is above 1. It then lets the session finish instead: `If the remaining work fits in a few more steps, finish it instead and skip these steps.` A session with more work left does the handoff steps now, before any other work, so a fresh session continues before auto-compaction has to. The nudge gives the operator the last word. If the operator has told the session not to hand off, it skips the note, continuity save and prepare steps, keeps working and leaves auto-compaction as the fallback. The nudge names a handoff note under `.skilled/skills/.state/session-handover/handoffs/` and a prepare command. The path resolves under the repository that holds the concern, so a runtime started in a subdirectory still writes under the repo root. The note needs flat frontmatter and four non-empty sections: Goal, Done, Remaining work and In-flight state. The Remaining work section lists only unfinished task work. In-flight state carries an `Active command:` line with the command, mode and arguments, or `none`. It also carries a `Running workers:` line, each worker as `pid <pid> log <path> collect <step>`, or `Running workers: none` when no worker runs.\n125: It also carries a `Scheduled:` line for each cron or wakeup with its schedule and prompt, or `Scheduled: none`. It carries an `Added directories:` line for each directory added after launch, or `Added directories: none`.\n126: The receiver runs `kill -0 <pid>` for each pid the `Running workers:` line lists, and when one is alive it collects that worker with the line's collect step instead of starting the same work again. After a hop it recreates each `Scheduled:` entry the note lists, or tells the operator which to recreate when the runtime has no scheduler, and re-adds each `Added directories:` entry, or asks the operator to re-add it, with `/add-dir <path>` on Claude Code. A Codex note also carries an `Active goal:` line with the goal or packet path, or `none`. Note creation, continuity saving and `prepare` are handoff steps. The nudge tells a session that holds a deep-loop lock to stop a live `fanout-run.cjs` runner first with `kill -TERM <runner-pid>`, wait until `kill -0 <runner-pid>` fails, then release the lock with the captured path, owner pid and nonce before `prepare`. `fanout-pool.cjs` probes the latest `spawned` pid in the ledger for each open lineage with signal 0 and `fanout-run.cjs` refuses a re-run with exit 3 while any lineage from an earlier run is still alive. When `spec_folder` is a packet path, Claude uses `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes send the save JSON to the [continuity writer](../../../.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js) with `--stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>` before prepare. `spec_folder` holds the repo-relative packet path, `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered. The nudge fills it from `SYSTEM_SPEC_FOLDER` when that names a valid packet and prints it empty otherwise, so the model never copies a placeholder that prepare rejects. A seed whose note path is not absolute adds nothing to the receiving session's context on any runtime.\n127: \n128: The `/speckit:handoff` command writes the note and runs `prepare`. The direct CLI form is:\n129: \n130: ~~~bash\n131: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs prepare --handoff <note-path> --session <session-id> --runtime <runtime>\n132: ~~~\n133: \n134: A missing `--handoff` exits 2 first. While the session-handover concern is off, prepare then exits 2 inside and outside `skilled-loop` without reading or changing the note, and its one line names the cause: a master or kill switch to unset, the runtime's own `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED` holding a falsy value, or no enable flag, when it names both `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED=1` and the runtime's flag. It arms no record and prints no claim command. `keep`, `claim`, `cancel`, `complete` and `history` do not check the switch. With the concern on, prepare validates the note, and an invalid note exits 2. Outside `skilled-loop`, it prints a manual claim command and exits 3 without checking for running shell children. Inside the loop, a live child of the runtime process exits 2 when its command name is `sh`, `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `dash`, `ksh`, `mksh`, `tcsh`, `csh`, `nu` or `pwsh`. A leading `-` is stripped before that lookup, so a login shell still matches. Any other child program is not detected. That includes a single simple command a tool shell replaces itself with: OpenCode runs `zsh -c 'sleep 45'` as a `sleep` child, so prepare goes ahead, while `sleep 45; echo done` keeps the shell and is refused. A walk over every descendant of the runtime was considered and declined, because the background worker it was proposed for has left the runtime's process tree by the time `prepare` runs. The note's `Running workers:` line covers work the session started itself. On Claude, Codex, Pi, OpenCode and Vibe, the arm withdrawal below and the killer's idle wait keep such a turn from being cut off. The runtime pid comes from `CLAUDE_PID` while that process descends from the loop. Otherwise, and on runtimes without the variable, the check resolves the runtime as the direct child of the loop on prepare's ancestor chain. When no runtime resolves, it logs `running-work-check-skipped` and skips. A shell that is prepare's own ancestor does not count. `--runtime` must ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n135: \n136: Every arm path asks the core whether the record is armable: its loop id, session id and runtime must match the arming session, where a record with no runtime counts as Claude's. A pending record arms. An armed record re-arms only when no killer is live for the loop, and the re-arm restamps `armedAt`, `armId` and the cwd, so a record whose killer died is fresh again when the next arm event fires. Stop matches the event session id to the stored record. A pending record stays pending when `transcript_path` is missing. The hook logs `missing-transcript-path`, stamps `deferredAt` on the record, prints one JSON line whose `systemMessage` says the next prompt withdraws the hop because the Stop carried no transcript path, and returns. While background tasks remain it leaves the record as it is, pending or armed, so a respawned killer never ends live work. A pending record it defers this way gains a `deferredAt` stamp. Each deferred Stop prints one JSON line whose `systemMessage` names the background task count and says the hop goes ahead at the first Stop after the tasks finish. It tells the operator to send a prompt or run `node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs cancel` to call the hop off. It carries no `decision` key, so Claude shows the notice and still ends the turn. When a record is armable and `payload.session_crons` is a non-empty array, Stop appends one `session-crons` line to `relaunch.log` with `loopId`, `sessionId` and `crons` set to the count. An empty, missing or non-array value writes nothing. A scheduled task and the todo list end with the session, so the receiver starts with neither. When the operator types a prompt in that session, the `UserPromptSubmit` nudge withdraws the session's record if it is deferred or already armed. The killer signals an armed session after a few quiet seconds, so a new turn that runs one long tool would otherwise be ended mid-turn and relaunched onto the old note. Claude, Codex, Pi, OpenCode and `cancel` a... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n137: \n138: Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At the next `turn_end` whose tool calls ran, those tools have finished, so it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit and steers the held text into the running loop with `deliverAs: 'steer'` and `triggerTurn: true`. The model reads it before its next call, as it reads a tool hook's context on Claude Code. A turn with no tool calls ends the loop, so `agent_end` makes the same checks and sends the text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` instead. Either way the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Pi's advisor, goal and spec-gate extensions append their own blocks to the user's prompt after a blank line, so the seed handler reads the claim seed from the prompt's first block. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the adapter at its real path so it is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record. When `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` is set but names no record, it keeps the nudge only while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n139: \n140: Pi and OpenCode both withdraw an arm when the session they armed starts a new turn. The OpenCode killer runs without `--transcript`, so it only waits its idle time. The Pi killer watches the session file, but Pi writes an assistant message only once it completes, so a turn that is still thinking looks idle. A turn the operator starts just after the arm would otherwise be signalled mid-turn. OpenCode disarms on a new user message and Pi on `agent_start`, each through the shared withdraw helper and only for a record whose `sessionId` is its own. That record is an armed one, or a pending one an arm event could not arm: when arming throws at `session.idle` or `agent_settled`, the plugin logs `session-idle-arm-error` or `agent-settled-arm-error` with `stage=arm` and stamps `deferredAt` on the record. A pending record no arm event has reached yet is left alone. The record ends `disarmed` with reason `user-message`, the session gets the extra nudge, the hop history lists the note as withdrawn, the plugin logs `arm-cancelled`, and the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record` and stands down. Both then tell the model, with the same withdrawn notice Claude and Codex send, because a model that asked for the hand-over otherwise still expects to end and goes idle with nothing armed. Pi steers it into the run from `agent_start`, which Pi awaits before the run's first model call. OpenCode adds it to the system prompt of every request in that turn, until the next operator prompt or a compaction, whose stale-note text replaces it. The note stays open and the wrapper prints no claim command for it, because `should-relaunch` reports the record as `withdrawn`. OpenCode also drops the nudge it held, so the extra nudge replaces the old one instead of repeating it on the next turn. The OpenCode goal continuation is suppressed while a relaunch record for the session is pending or armed, because its idle turn would send the user message that withdraws the prepared hop.\n141: \n142: A question dialog holds the turn or run open until the operator answers, so a session that asks after `prepare` never reaches the event that arms its record. Claude Code, Pi and OpenCode therefore refuse a question dialog while the session's record can still be armed: a pending record, or an armed one with no live killer. Claude denies `AskUserQuestion` in `PreToolUse`, Pi blocks its `question` and `ask_user_question` tools in `tool_call`, and OpenCode throws from `tool.execute.before` for its `question` tool. All three take the reason from `questionHoldReason` in the shared nudge module, which names the note and the `cancel` command. Codex needs no guard: its question tool in a default session is `request_user_input_async`, which does not hold the turn, so the turn ends and the hop runs, and the unanswered question ends with the old session.\n143: \n144: The shared nudge module gives every adapter the note path and the same suppression rules. Outside `skilled-loop`, with `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` unset or empty, the nudge never asks for `prepare`, because prepare could only print a manual claim command and a session that stopped there would wait for a hand-off nobody runs. It asks for the note and `keep` instead, as Outside The Loop below describes. A re-nudge reuses the note this session already has open, matched on `session_id` and a `status` of `open`, and only takes a new name when no such note exists. A new note is named `<UTC stamp>-<session id>.md` with the full id, because OpenCode and Pi ids start with a shared time-based prefix and two sessions nudged in the same second would otherwise plan the same note. An open note under the older eight-character name is still found and reused. A record that is pending, or armed and still inside `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_AGE_MIN`, means the handoff steps already ran, so no adapter nudges. An armed record past the age limit is stale, cannot relaunch and no longer silences the nudge. The age limit resolves the way the wrapper resolves its switches, from the hook config file with the environment taking precedence. On Claude Code the nudge stays silent for a tool call that carries an `agent_id`, because a sub-agent or fork reports its parent's session and transcript, so its fill and its note would be the parent's.\n145: \n146: Inside the loop, no adapter nudges once `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` reaches `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_HOPS`, because a record prepared there could not relaunch. Every seed handler reads the note path back out of the seed, so the claim instructions it adds name the real note.\n147: \n148: Two hooks can decide on one nudge at once. Claude Code and Codex run a hook per tool call in a parallel batch, and a prompt hook beside a tool hook, and Vibe runs tool calls side by side. So the Claude, Codex and Vibe nudges deliver through `commitNudge` in the shared nudge module. Only the hook that creates the session's claim file in the state directory delivers, and it decides again under that claim before it writes the level, because a hook that delivered first has already written its own. A hook that finds the claim held stays quiet. A claim older than 10 seconds was left by a hook that died and is taken over.\n149: \n150: Codex reads the latest `token_count` record in the rollout named by the payload's `transcript_path` and nudges through `additionalContext` on `PostToolUse`. Its `UserPromptSubmit` entry point handles seed prompts and runs the same nudge check, because Codex binds each script to one event. Its `Stop` adapter accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop` and passes `transcript_path` to the killer. Codex runs its `SessionStart` hooks again with `source: compact` after it compacts a root session, before the next model request, so `codex/compact-pointer.mjs` resets the nudge level there and points the session at its open note, or sends the stale-note text for a withdrawn one, as Claude's pointer does. Claude and Codex write the armed state before starting the killer, so a killer spawn failure does not return the record to pending.\n151: \n152: Vibe reads fill from its journal and nudges from `turn-start` on `pre_agent` and from `compaction-post-tool` on every `post_tool`. Vibe runs tool calls side by side, so its nudge goes through the shared nudge claim described above. The `pre_agent` and `post_agent` hooks are registered for the `main` alias, which Vibe gives root sessions and forks, so a forked or rewound session keeps its hand-over. Only the unscoped `post_tool` path skips a payload with a `parent_session_id`. The first `turn-start` part withdraws an armed record before the slower parts run, and records the session id for the loop so `session-id --runtime vibe` can print it. `turn-end` on `post_agent` arms the record and hands the killer the session's whole `journal/` folder, because Vibe starts a new segment at the fold that ends the turn. The killer treats a folder as active while the folder or any numbered segment in it changes. A turn end that finds this session's pending record but cannot arm it marks the record deferred, so the next operator prompt withdraws it. After a compaction the Vibe part resets the nudge level, prints no claim pointer while a hop is pending, and sends the stale-note text for a withdrawn one.\n153: \n154: \n155: Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record that has no live killer owner: Claude Stop, Codex Stop, OpenCode `session.idle` and Pi `agent_settled`. It does nothing while a killer owns the lock. A record left armed by a failed spawn is therefore recovered by the next arm event.\n156: \n157: The killer polls transcript mtime every 100 ms. When the transcript stays unchanged for `--idle-ms` (3000 ms by default), it confirms the target is a descendant of the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is still armed, below the hop cap and still fresh at the end of the SIGTERM grace period by the wrapper's own age rule. At or above the cap it unarms the record with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and sends neither a signal nor a notification. A record that would be stale by then is unarmed with reason `stale` and logged as `killer-stale`, so the killer never ends a session the wrapper would refuse to relaunch. The OpenCode plugin runs on Bun, so it starts the killer with `node` instead of `process.execPath`, which points to the OpenCode CLI. It hands the killer the loop's direct child rather than its own pid, because OpenCode 2 loads plugins in a server its TUI starts, and signalling that server would leave the TUI running. Just before the signal the killer stamps `signalledAt` on the record, in the same locked step as its last check that the record is still the arm it watched, and a new arm drops the stamp. The `--loop-id` value must be greater than 1. If the record is no longer armed, the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`. If the target exits during the SIGTERM call, it logs `target-gone`.\n158: \n159: The idle deadline (`--timeout-ms`, 30000 ms by default) does not shorten the 15000 ms grace period after SIGTERM, which leaves room for an OpenCode 2 TUI that was measured taking about six seconds to exit. If the idle deadline passes before the transcript is idle, or the target stays alive through the grace period, the killer disarms the record with reason `killer-timeout` and attempts a macOS notification with the manual claim command. Its title reads `Session handover needs attention (killer timed out)`, and when `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE` is unusable a subtitle says the template was ignored and gives the reason.\n160: \n161: A single failed transcript read is not a vanish. The killer gives up only when the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break (`TRANSCRIPT_MISSING_GRACE_MS`). That give-up follows the timeout path: the record is disarmed with `killer-timeout` and the notification is attempted.\n162: \n163: ### Known Race: A Claude Goal Continuation During The Arm\n164: \n165: A Claude sender under an unmet native goal usually still hops, because no `UserPromptSubmit` fires for the continuation and the killer's 3 s idle wait lands in its first API call. The hop is lost with `killer-timeout` only when the continuation keeps writing less than 3 s apart for 30 s. A continuation tool call longer than 3 s can be killed mid-run, and that work is not in the note.\n166: \n167: The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 15000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid and token of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer writes its pid and a random token to the mutex `owner` file. On release it renames the directory to a private `.stale-` name and removes it only when the inode it recorded and the token both still match, and otherwise renames it back. One window remains, and it needs three killers racing on the same loop's takeover: if a third killer recreates the mutex path while a mismatched directory is being renamed back, the displaced directory stays under its `.stale-` name until the stale sweep, and while it does, two killers can both pass the re-check, one can remove the lock the other just created with its exclusive open, and both then su... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n168: \n169: After the selected runtime exits, `skilled-loop` declines unless the record is armed, fresh and below the hop limit. It consumes an eligible record, increments the hop count and launches that runtime with a seed that carries the Gate 3 answer:\n170: \n171: ~~~text\n172: [session-handover] claim and continue handoff <note-path> | Spec folder: <spec-folder> (carried from the handoff; Gate 3 answered in the sending session)\n173: [session-handover] claim and continue handoff <note-path> | Spec folder: none (Gate 3 answered D, Skip, in the sending session)\n174: ~~~\n175: \n176: If the record is still pending when the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` prints the runtime-specific manual claim command on stdout and preserves the runtime's exit code. The core fills that command from `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE`. When a carried model, name, provider or Pi extension value contains the template's `@@HANDOFF@@` placeholder, the wrapper prints an error naming the value on stderr, then the plain command, and turns a clean runtime exit into exit 1. The CLI's `prepare` warns on stderr the same way when the template holds no placeholder or more than one. For a valid repo-relative `spec_folder`, the wrapper adds a `Spec folder:` suffix with the packet path. When the field is `none`, it adds a Skip suffix. An empty field adds no suffix, so the receiver asks Gate 3 as usual. A valid path carries the sending session's answer across the hop. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiver claims the note and reads its four sections. When the packet holds a `goal.md`, it reads that file before Remaining work. When In-flight state names an `Active command:` other than `none`, it runs that command again with the same mode and arguments. When the note names an `Active goal:` other than `none`, it calls `create_goal`, or tells the operator which goal to set on a runtime without it. For a packet path, it runs `/speckit:resume`. For `none`, it skips resume and treats Gate 3 as answered D) Skip. It works only on the task's Remaining work. It never runs `prepare` for the claimed note. It runs `complete` when the task work ends:\n177: \n178: ~~~bash\n179: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs claim <note-path> --session <new-session-id>\n180: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs complete <note-path>\n181: ~~~\n182: \n183: ### Goal Packet\n184: \n185: A note can carry an optional `goal_packet` frontmatter field: the repo-relative directory of the packet whose `goal.md` holds the sending session's goal. `prepare` and `keep` own the field. Each call resolves the sender's goal again and writes the result: the packet when the sender has a goal that can be proven, the packet goal of the note's spec folder for a Claude sender that never had a goal set, and no field at all otherwise. A value already in the note is replaced or removed, so a note prepared again after its goal ended stops handing the goal on, and a value written by hand does not survive. A Claude goal that ended also leaves a `goal_ended: true` field in the note, written and removed by the same two commands. Where the goal is found depends on the sender's runtime:\n186: \n187: | Sender | Where the goal is read |\n188: |---|---|\n189: | OpenCode, Pi, Devin, Cursor | The session's active goal record under `.skilled/skills/.state/goal/`, which names the bound packet. A paused, completed or cleared goal counts as none. Devin and Cursor have no record under the fresh native id a manual claim starts, so they also keep the packet the claim exported in `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` when it names a real packet. |\n190: | Vibe | No goal record. The packet the Vibe goal hook renders from: `VIBE_SPEC_FOLDER` when it is set, otherwise the `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` a claim exported, either one only when it names a real packet. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 191]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md, lines 1-190\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Session Handover Hook: Context-Limit Handoff and Fresh-Session Handover\"\n3: description: \"Shared core, per-runtime adapters, CLI and skilled-loop wrapper that hand unfinished work to a fresh session in the same terminal when a runtime nears its context limit.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"session handover hook\"\n6:   - \"skilled-loop relaunch\"\n7:   - \"context limit handoff\"\n8: ---\n9: # Session Handover\n10: \n11: > Shared adapters and the `skilled-loop` wrapper carry unfinished work into a fresh runtime session.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: The `session-handover/` folder holds the shared handoff logic, one adapter per automatic runtime, the CLI and the tests for the session handover loop. It also holds the history lookup: `bin/history-lookup.mjs`, the runnable entry for the printed lookup command, and `history/`, the engine that reads the sender's earlier turns back. Section 3 explains how the lookup works end to end. Each adapter asks for a handoff near its context threshold. The sender prepares a note and the adapter arms a matching record. After the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` relaunches it with a claim seed when the record is armed, fresh and below the hop cap. If a record is still pending at exit, the wrapper prints a manual claim command for the selected runtime and consumes the record. `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE` builds that command from the first launch's name and Pi extension flags, and from the model, provider and effort the record carries when it has them, so after a hop that switched model the printed command names the model the session was last using. The killer uses the same template in its timeout notification.\n18: \n19: Claude Code uses `PreToolUse` for Bash, `PostToolUse`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop` and `SessionStart`, both after a compaction and at startup for the claim of a hop whose start prompt is the goal command. Pi, OpenCode, Codex and Vibe use their host events or a plugin. Cursor and Devin ship on the command trigger only. Start every runtime through `skilled-loop` for the loop, by hand or through the shell shim in section 2. Inside the loop the nudge asks for a hop. Outside it, the nudge asks only for a note the session keeps through auto-compaction, as section 3 describes under Outside The Loop.\n20: \n21: ### Runtime adapter matrix\n22: \n23: | Runtime | Trigger | Relaunch seed syntax | Model carry | Mirror form | Spike evidence | Verdict |\n24: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n25: | Claude | automatic | `claude [--model <model>] \"<seed>\" [--name <name>]` | `--model`, `--model=` | [.claude/settings.json](../../../.claude/settings.json) hook entries | [SPIKE-REPORT.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/001-claude-relaunch-loop/scratch/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md) | AUTO |\n26: | Pi | automatic | `pi [--provider <p>] [--model <model>] [-ne] [-e <path>] [--name <name>] \"<seed>\"` | `--model`, `--provider`, extension flags | `.pi/extensions/` one-line entry file that re-exports `pi/session-handover.ts` | [SPIKE-REPORT.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/002-pi-opencode-adapters/scratch/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md) | AUTO |\n27: | OpenCode | automatic | `opencode [-m <model>] --prompt \"<seed>\"`, and on OpenCode 2 `opencode --standalone --prompt \"<seed>\"` | `-m`, `--model`, `--model=` on OpenCode 1 only, because the OpenCode 2 root command has no model flag | `.opencode/plugins/` plugin plus `opencode/` browsability symlink | [SPIKE-REPORT.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/002-pi-opencode-adapters/scratch/spike/SPIKE-REPORT.md) | AUTO |\n28: | Codex | automatic | `codex --no-daemon [-m <model>] \"<seed>\"` | `-m`, `--model`, `--model=` | [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json) hook entries plus `.codex/hooks/` symlinks | [SPIKE-codex.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/003-command-trigger-runtimes/scratch/spike/SPIKE-codex.md) | AUTO |\n29: | Cursor | `/speckit-handoff` | `cursor-agent [--model <model>] \"<seed>\"` | `--model`, `--model=` | [.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md](../../../.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md) symlink | Cursor spike, packet `023-session-relaunch-loop/003` | COMMAND-ONLY |\n30: | Devin | `/speckit:handoff` | `devin [--model <model>] -- \"<seed>\"` | `--model`, `--model=` | none, [.devin/SYNC.md](../../../.devin/SYNC.md) carries no command surface | [SPIKE-devin.md](../../../specs/hooks-plugins-extensions/session-handover/002-session-handover-loop/003-command-trigger-runtimes/scratch/spike/SPIKE-devin.md) | COMMAND-ONLY |\n31: | Vibe | automatic from a context nudge, and under `skilled-loop` a typed `/speckit-handoff` arms too, because the session reads its id from the `session-id` command; outside the loop it uses a `uuidgen` id that is never armed, so that route prints the claim command | `vibe \"<seed>\"` | none, because `--model` is headless-only | [.vibe/hooks.toml](../../../.vibe/hooks.toml) entries for `turn-start`, `turn-end` and `compaction-post-tool`, plus [.vibe/prompts/speckit-handoff.md](../../../.vibe/prompts/speckit-handoff.md) for the manual start | [resume-probe.md](../../../specs/cli-external-orchestration/runtime-lifecycle/004-cli-mistral-vibe-creation/019-vibe-relaunch-command-tier/scratch/resume-probe.md) | AUTO |\n32: \n33: `skilled-loop` exports `SKILLED_LOOP_RUNTIME` with the selected runtime. It carries the first launch's model for every runtime. Pi also carries its provider. Claude and Pi carry their name. Pi carries its extension flags. Relative Pi extension paths resolve against the wrapper's starting directory. `prepare` and `claim` require `--session <id>`. Claude can omit it and use `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. Vibe under `skilled-loop` can omit it too: Vibe gives its tools no session id, so the turn-start hook records the id it sees for the loop, and `session-id --runtime vibe` prints it. Other runtimes pass the id directly. Runtimes with no session-id variable, such as Devin, Cursor or Vibe outside the loop, run `uuidgen` once and reuse the value. `CLAUDE_PID` supplies Claude's process id for the shell-child check, and Stop keeps it as the killer target while it descends from the loop. `complete` requires only the note path.\n34: \n35: ### Model And Effort Across A Hop\n36: \n37: A relaunched session starts on the model and effort the sender was using, where the runtime can be told them at launch. The wrapper replays the first launch's model and effort flags on every relaunch, in each runtime's own syntax. At `prepare` the CLI also reads the sender's latest values from its own session log on Claude and Codex and from the CLI config on Cursor, and the Pi extension adds them when it arms. All of them keep the values on the relaunch record, never in the note. `consume` hands them to the wrapper, and a value read from the log beats the launch flag for that hop. The value that was used then carries to later hops that record none.\n38: \n39: Model and effort move as one set, because an effort is chosen for one model and another model may reject it. When the hop's model differs from the launch model and the record carries no usable effort, the wrapper drops the launch effort and the receiver starts on the runtime's default effort. An arm that records a new model without an effort removes the effort the record held. A launch with no model flag has no model to compare, so its effort stands, and a live model equal to the launch model keeps the launch effort too.\n40: \n41: - **Claude:** model from `--model`, effort from `--effort` (`low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`). The session log's last main-chain assistant entry supplies `requestedModel`, or the response model, and `effort`. A `/model` or `/effort` switch is written to the log as soon as it is confirmed, as a local command entry and its stdout, and a switch confirmed after that assistant entry wins for its field, so it reaches the relaunch without waiting for another turn. `/effort` takes the level from its `Set effort level to <level>` line. `/model` takes the id typed after it; the picker leaves that empty and confirms with a display name such as `Opus 5.5`, which proves a switch but not an id, so the model reads as unproven and the launch model stands. A new model drops the old turn's effort, while an `/effort` switch of its own still counts. A cancelled command changes nothing.\n42: - **Codex:** model from `-m`, effort from `-c model_reasoning_effort=<level>` or `--config`, replayed as `-c model_reasoning_effort=\"<level>\"`. The rollout's last `turn_context` supplies `model` and `effort`.\n43: - **Pi:** model and provider as before, effort from `--thinking` (`off` up to `max`). A `:<thinking>` suffix on the model travels inside the model. The extension hands the session's current model, provider and thinking level to the record when it arms it. The provider is used only together with a live model the wrapper accepted. A launch `--model openai/gpt-6-luna` and a recorded `openai` plus `gpt-6-luna` are the same model, so that hop keeps the launch thinking level when the record has none. A recorded provider that differs from the launch provider, named by `--provider X` or as `X/model`, counts as a model switch, so that hop drops the launch thinking level when the record has none. A launch that names no provider has nothing to compare, so a provider change on the same model keeps the launch thinking level. When the plan-build extension is loaded, the sender's Plan or Build mode is replayed too: the hop starts with `--plan` or `--build` for the mode the sender was last in, in place of the launch flag.\n44: - **Cursor:** the model, with any bracketed `effort=` inside it. At `prepare` the CLI reads the current `model` from `~/.cursor/cli-config.json`. When `selectedModel` names the same model, its parameters are added as `<modelId>[<id>=<value>,...]`. Cursor rewrites `model` on every switch but `selectedModel` only for a parameterized choice, so a `selectedModel` naming another model is stale and is ignored. The file is shared by every Cursor session, so the CLI uses it only when it changed after the wrapper launched this hop, which the wrapper records as `SKILLED_LOOP_LAUNCHED_AT`. An older file may hold another session's choice, and the auto model has no id to pass, so in either case the launch flag stands.\n45: - **Devin:** the model only, because it has no effort flag. It reads no live value.\n46: - **OpenCode 2:** nothing, because its root command takes no model or effort flag. The receiver starts on the configured default.\n47: \n48: The Claude Stop adapter stamps a non-empty `payload.permission_mode` on the armed record as `permissionMode`, and `consume` prints it. A Claude hop and the manual claim command carry a usable live mode as `--permission-mode`. A launch `--permission-mode` is captured into `SKILLED_LOOP_PERMISSION_MODE`, so a hop with no usable live mode keeps the launch value. On a hop, a live `plan` mode keeps the launch mode and prints `note: the sender was in plan mode, the receiver starts in the launch mode` on stderr. A live `bypassPermissions` is replayed only when the launch line already carried `--dangerously-skip-permissions` or `--allow-dangerously-skip-permissions`, and the wrapper never adds an unlocking flag of its own. On a hop, any other live mode the wrapper cannot replay keeps the launch mode with a `warning:` line on stderr. Codex, Pi, OpenCode, Cursor and Devin record no permission mode, so a live permission mode never reaches their sessions.\n49: \n50: Claude and Pi carry the session name across a hop. On Claude, `prepare` records the last `custom-title` entry's `customTitle` from the session log as `name` ahead of `SKILLED_LOOP_NAME`, so a `/rename` reaches the receiver. A session with no rename and no launch name records the last `ai-title` entry's `aiTitle`, the title Claude generated, so the receiver keeps that title with a number and is not given a new one. Pi carries the name from its launch line. The wrapper strips the number it wrote for the sender, exports the stem and relaunches with `--name` set to the stem plus the next number, so the first receiver is `<stem> (2)` and every later receiver counts up without the suffixes stacking. On a hop, a record name that starts with `-` or holds a control character is ignored with a `warning:` line on stderr and the launch name stands. A manual claim prints the stem with no number.\n51: \n52: A level outside the runtime's list is dropped with a `warning:` line on stderr, and a logged model that does not look like a model id is ignored the same way, so a bad value never stops a relaunch.\n53: \n54: ### Automatic triggers on Codex\n55: \n56: Codex binds four repo hooks rendered from `hook-registry.json` into [.codex/hooks.json](../../../.codex/hooks.json). `prompt-seed.mjs` binds `UserPromptSubmit`: a `[session-handover]` prompt receives the claim-seed instructions, and a non-seed prompt first withdraws a record this session armed or a failed Stop deferred, as the Stop paragraph below describes, then runs the same threshold check as `context-nudge.mjs`, which binds `PostToolUse`. The Codex prompt payload carries only the prompt text, so any prompt other than a seed counts as the operator's. The Codex hook installer allows each script under one event only, so seed detection and the mid-session nudge are separate entry points over `shared.mjs`. The fill comes from the transcript rollout's latest `token_count` event: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` is the numerator and `cached_input_tokens` stays out because it is already inside that count. The window is `SESSION_HANDOVER_WINDOW_TOKENS`, then the event's `model_context_window`. The nudge reaches the model as `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, the envelope the repo's other Codex adapters emit. `stop-arm.mjs` accepts only payloads whose `hook_event_name` is `Stop`. It resolves the target, arms a matching pending record, then spawns the killer with `--transcript <transcript_path>`. A spawn failure leaves the record armed until the next Stop restarts its killer. A Stop that cannot arm, because it carried no transcript path, found no session process below the loop or the arm threw, stamps `deferredAt` on the pending record and prints one `systemMessage` that names the cause and says the next prompt withdraws the hand-over. `compact-pointer.mjs` binds `SessionStart` and acts only on `source: compact`. Codex exports no pid variable, so the killer target resolves through the same ancestor rule the CLI's running-work check uses. `skilled-loop codex` always starts Codex with `--no-daemon` because its shared app-server daemon keeps the environment of the session... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n57: \n58: \n59: ### Command-only runtimes: Cursor and Devin\n60: \n61: Cursor ships on the command trigger for two reasons. Its spike evidence is source-only: the bundle's hook dispatch code was inspected, but no live payload was captured because the machine's Cursor credentials were expired and no permitted provider exists for Cursor. And `stop` is not wired in this repository's [.cursor/hooks.json](../../../.cursor/hooks.json), whose registered event list has no `stop`. Devin ships on the command trigger because its hook usage is unverified: the historical probe summarizes field names without a raw payload and current `SessionEnd` behavior is unknown, so an automatic arm cannot be proven.\n62: \n63: Start a handoff by running `/speckit:handoff` inside the session. It writes the note and runs `prepare`. Outside `skilled-loop`, prepare prints the manual claim command and exits 3, and that line starts the hop by hand. While the concern is off, prepare refuses in both places, as section 3 describes. Outside the loop a session runs this command only when the operator asks for a manual handoff. Otherwise it keeps working. The printed command uses the selected runtime's seed form: Claude, Pi, Codex and Cursor use the seed as a positional argument, OpenCode adds `--prompt`, and Devin adds `--` before the seed. `!` in a handoff path sits in single quotes outside the seed's double quotes. For example, the CLI and wrapper print `...handoff /tmp/han\"'!'\"doff.md`, which interactive Bash preserves when history expansion is on. Cursor names a project command after its file, so in Cursor the command is `/speckit-handoff`, reached through [.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md](../../../.cursor/commands/speckit-handoff.md), a symlink to the canonical file. Devin has no command surface, so a Devin session reads [.skilled/commands/speckit/handoff.md](../../commands/speckit/handoff.md) directly and follows it. Under `skilled-loop`, a record prepared on a command-only runtime stays pending because no arm event exists for it, so the hop runs by launching the wrapper with the claim seed. On Cursor and Devin a successful prepare says the handoff is prepared and nothing ends this session, and tells the session to exit and run the claim command the wrapper prints.\n64: \n65: ### Runtime Behavior And Limits\n66: \n67: `SESSION_HANDOVER_THRESHOLD` controls when each session is nudged. Keep the threshold well above a fresh session's startup fill plus its remaining work. Codex live runs at `0.12` chained hop after hop because each new session reached about 10% on startup alone. `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_HOPS` caps completed relaunches and stops the chain once that limit is reached.\n68: \n69: This repository's Pi setup needs `-ne -e` and the [.pi/extensions/session-handover.ts](../../../.pi/extensions/session-handover.ts) extension. Two installed Pi extensions both register `--fast`, so the default startup conflicts. The wrapper carries the selected extension flags across hops.\n70: \n71: In the recorded live runs, OpenCode reported about 1M context tokens for `openai/gpt-6-luna`. Pi reported 272K for the same model. The live handoff results and operator inputs are in the [Pi and OpenCode playbook](../../skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/plugins-and-hooks/session-handover-pi-opencode.md).\n72: \n73: ---\n74: \n75: ## 2. STARTING THE LOOP\n76: \n77: Start each runtime from the repository root through the wrapper:\n78: \n79: ~~~bash\n80: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop claude \"Work on the task and preserve unfinished work for the next session.\"\n81: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop pi --provider openai --model gpt-6-luna -ne -e .pi/extensions/session-handover.ts\n82: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop opencode -m openai/gpt-6-luna\n83: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop codex -m gpt-6-luna\n84: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop cursor\n85: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop devin\n86: bash .skilled/bin/skilled-loop vibe\n87: ~~~\n88: \n89: The wrapper accepts `skilled-loop [claude|pi|opencode|codex|cursor|devin|vibe] [args...]` and defaults to `claude`. Vibe's `--model` is headless-only, so a hop never replays it and the interactive relaunch keeps the configured `active_model`. The `cursor` arm launches the `cursor-agent` binary. A hop replays the first launch's options in their original order and replaces the start prompt with the claim seed. Model, effort and name come from the relaunch record when it carries a usable value for that field and from the launch line otherwise. The Pi provider comes from the record only together with a recorded model the wrapper accepted and from the launch provider otherwise. Pi extension settings come from the first launch's arguments. Flags that select or continue a session, including Claude `-c`, `--resume`, `--session-id` and `--cloud`, Pi `--session` and `--fork`, OpenCode `--session` and `--prompt`, Codex `--image`, `--worktree` and `--last`, Devin `--resume`, `--prompt-file` and `--print`, and Cursor `--worktree` and `--worktree-base`, are in the deny list and are not repeated. Options documented by each runtime's help are replayed with their values, including repeated options. If an unknown option is followed by a bare word, the wrapper warns and declines the hop because it cannot distinguish an option value from the start prompt. Relative Pi extension paths are resolved against the starting directory before a hop changes directories.\n90: \n91: The wrapper exports one loop id and starts `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` at 0. It relaunches only when a matching record is armed, fresh and below the configured hop limit.\n92: \n93: ### Run every session under the loop\n94: \n95: The session-handover hooks ship on for Claude Code and off for every other runtime. Set `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED=1` in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` or the environment to turn them on everywhere, or turn one runtime on or off with its own `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED` (section 5). Without an enable flag only Claude Code sessions are nudged, and auto-compaction stays the fallback on the others.\n96: \n97: Then add one line to `~/.zshrc` or `~/.bashrc`, with the absolute path of the checkout that owns `.skilled`:\n98: \n99: ~~~bash\n100: source /path/to/repo/.skilled/bin/skilled-loop-shell.sh\n101: ~~~\n102: \n103: The shim defines `claude`, `pi`, `opencode`, `codex`, `devin`, `cursor-agent` and `skilled-loop` as shell functions. The `skilled-loop` function runs the wrapper from the checkout that owns the sourced shim, so a pasted manual claim works from any directory and without the terminal check. Typing `claude` in a terminal then runs `bash <repo>/.skilled/bin/skilled-loop claude`, and `cursor-agent` runs the wrapper's `cursor` arm. A function hands the launch to the wrapper only when all of these hold:\n104: \n105: - `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` is unset, so no loop is running yet.\n106: - Standard input and standard output are both a terminal.\n107: - The arguments open an interactive session. Subcommands such as `claude doctor`, `codex exec` or `opencode run`, print modes such as `-p`, version and help run the binary directly. Each list comes from that runtime's own `--help`. Codex `resume` and `fork`, and Cursor `agent`, `ls` and `resume` stay wrapped, because they open a session.\n108: - A `.skilled/bin/skilled-loop` exists in the current directory or one of its parents. A `.skilled` that is a symlink counts, so the wrapper also starts in a repository that links this checkout's `.skilled`.\n109: \n110: The shim resolves only wrappers inside the sourced checkout, a linked worktree registered to it, or an absolute checkout listed in `SKILLED_LOOP_TRUSTED_CHECKOUTS` as a colon-separated value. An unset or empty variable trusts no extra checkout.\n111: \n112: The automatic hop in a linking repository also needs that repository's runtime folders, because each runtime reads its hooks or plugins from the project it starts in. Link `.claude`, `.codex`, `.opencode` and `.pi` from this checkout as well, or give each its own session-handover registration. A repository that links only `.skilled` starts under the wrapper but never receives the nudge, so its sessions hop only through `/speckit:handoff`.\n113: \n114: A wrapper runs the code it read when it started, so a change to `.skilled/bin/skilled-loop` reaches no hop of a loop that is already running. The wrapper takes a checksum of its own file before the first launch and compares it before each hop. When the file changed, it prints one line on stderr: `note: skilled-loop changed on disk after this loop started, restart the wrapper to run the new code`. It also appends a `wrapper-changed` line with the loop id to `relaunch.log`, so an unattended hop that scrolls the note away still leaves a record. A checksum that comes back empty, because a checkout replaced the file for a moment, is not a change and prints and logs nothing. The hop still goes ahead on the code the loop started with. Exit the session and start the runtime again to pick the change up.\n115: \n116: In every other case the function runs `command <binary> \"$@\"`. `skilled_loop_resolve <runtime> [args...]` prints the wrapper path the shim would use, or nothing. It skips the terminal check, so you can call it from a script to see the decision. Claude, Pi, OpenCode and Codex then hop on their own near the context threshold. Cursor and Devin run under the wrapper too, but they hop only after `/speckit:handoff`, as section 1 describes. An alias with the same name still takes precedence over the function, so remove a `claude` alias before you rely on the shim. Agent tool shells, such as Claude Code's, are rebuilt from a function snapshot that drops names starting with a single underscore, so every helper the shim's functions call keeps a plain `skilled_loop_` name.\n117: \n118: ---\n119: \n120: ## 3. HANDOFF PROTOCOL\n121: \n122: Claude's `context-nudge` handles `PostToolUse` and `UserPromptSubmit`. It reads the latest main-chain assistant usage from the Claude transcript and compares the fill ratio with the configured threshold. The default threshold is 0.80. One more nudge fires 0.05 above that level. Each level fires once. A withdrawn or cancelled hand-over earns one extra nudge, as the Stop paragraph below describes. The adapter accepts session ids matching `[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,128}`. Transcript read errors produce no usage. The reader starts with the last 256 KiB of the transcript. When that tail holds no assistant row, for example after a tool result larger than the tail, it widens the read up to 16 MiB before it reports no usage. The Codex reader widens the same way. Failed Claude settings reads are treated as absent.\n123: \n124: The nudge reports the fill as `[session-handover] Context is about N% full.`, or as `[session-handover] Context is past the configured window (over 100% full).` when the ratio is above 1. It then lets the session finish instead: `If the remaining work fits in a few more steps, finish it instead and skip these steps.` A session with more work left does the handoff steps now, before any other work, so a fresh session continues before auto-compaction has to. The nudge gives the operator the last word. If the operator has told the session not to hand off, it skips the note, continuity save and prepare steps, keeps working and leaves auto-compaction as the fallback. The nudge names a handoff note under `.skilled/skills/.state/session-handover/handoffs/` and a prepare command. The path resolves under the repository that holds the concern, so a runtime started in a subdirectory still writes under the repo root. The note needs flat frontmatter and four non-empty sections: Goal, Done, Remaining work and In-flight state. The Remaining work section lists only unfinished task work. In-flight state carries an `Active command:` line with the command, mode and arguments, or `none`. It also carries a `Running workers:` line, each worker as `pid <pid> log <path> collect <step>`, or `Running workers: none` when no worker runs.\n125: It also carries a `Scheduled:` line for each cron or wakeup with its schedule and prompt, or `Scheduled: none`. It carries an `Added directories:` line for each directory added after launch, or `Added directories: none`.\n126: The receiver runs `kill -0 <pid>` for each pid the `Running workers:` line lists, and when one is alive it collects that worker with the line's collect step instead of starting the same work again. After a hop it recreates each `Scheduled:` entry the note lists, or tells the operator which to recreate when the runtime has no scheduler, and re-adds each `Added directories:` entry, or asks the operator to re-add it, with `/add-dir <path>` on Claude Code. A Codex note also carries an `Active goal:` line with the goal or packet path, or `none`. Note creation, continuity saving and `prepare` are handoff steps. The nudge tells a session that holds a deep-loop lock to stop a live `fanout-run.cjs` runner first with `kill -TERM <runner-pid>`, wait until `kill -0 <runner-pid>` fails, then release the lock with the captured path, owner pid and nonce before `prepare`. `fanout-pool.cjs` probes the latest `spawned` pid in the ledger for each open lineage with signal 0 and `fanout-run.cjs` refuses a re-run with exit 3 while any lineage from an earlier run is still alive. When `spec_folder` is a packet path, Claude uses `/speckit:save`. Other runtimes send the save JSON to the [continuity writer](../../../.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js) with `--stdin --full-auto <spec-folder>` before prepare. `spec_folder` holds the repo-relative packet path, `none` when Gate 3 was answered D) Skip or empty when Gate 3 was never answered. The nudge fills it from `SYSTEM_SPEC_FOLDER` when that names a valid packet and prints it empty otherwise, so the model never copies a placeholder that prepare rejects. A seed whose note path is not absolute adds nothing to the receiving session's context on any runtime.\n127: \n128: The `/speckit:handoff` command writes the note and runs `prepare`. The direct CLI form is:\n129: \n130: ~~~bash\n131: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs prepare --handoff <note-path> --session <session-id> --runtime <runtime>\n132: ~~~\n133: \n134: A missing `--handoff` exits 2 first. While the session-handover concern is off, prepare then exits 2 inside and outside `skilled-loop` without reading or changing the note, and its one line names the cause: a master or kill switch to unset, the runtime's own `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_<RUNTIME>_ENABLED` holding a falsy value, or no enable flag, when it names both `SYSTEM_SESSION_HANDOVER_ENABLED=1` and the runtime's flag. It arms no record and prints no claim command. `keep`, `claim`, `cancel`, `complete` and `history` do not check the switch. With the concern on, prepare validates the note, and an invalid note exits 2. Outside `skilled-loop`, it prints a manual claim command and exits 3 without checking for running shell children. Inside the loop, a live child of the runtime process exits 2 when its command name is `sh`, `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `dash`, `ksh`, `mksh`, `tcsh`, `csh`, `nu` or `pwsh`. A leading `-` is stripped before that lookup, so a login shell still matches. Any other child program is not detected. That includes a single simple command a tool shell replaces itself with: OpenCode runs `zsh -c 'sleep 45'` as a `sleep` child, so prepare goes ahead, while `sleep 45; echo done` keeps the shell and is refused. A walk over every descendant of the runtime was considered and declined, because the background worker it was proposed for has left the runtime's process tree by the time `prepare` runs. The note's `Running workers:` line covers work the session started itself. On Claude, Codex, Pi, OpenCode and Vibe, the arm withdrawal below and the killer's idle wait keep such a turn from being cut off. The runtime pid comes from `CLAUDE_PID` while that process descends from the loop. Otherwise, and on runtimes without the variable, the check resolves the runtime as the direct child of the loop on prepare's ancestor chain. When no runtime resolves, it logs `running-work-check-skipped` and skips. A shell that is prepare's own ancestor does not count. `--runtime` must ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n135: \n136: Every arm path asks the core whether the record is armable: its loop id, session id and runtime must match the arming session, where a record with no runtime counts as Claude's. A pending record arms. An armed record re-arms only when no killer is live for the loop, and the re-arm restamps `armedAt`, `armId` and the cwd, so a record whose killer died is fresh again when the next arm event fires. Stop matches the event session id to the stored record. A pending record stays pending when `transcript_path` is missing. The hook logs `missing-transcript-path`, stamps `deferredAt` on the record, prints one JSON line whose `systemMessage` says the next prompt withdraws the hop because the Stop carried no transcript path, and returns. While background tasks remain it leaves the record as it is, pending or armed, so a respawned killer never ends live work. A pending record it defers this way gains a `deferredAt` stamp. Each deferred Stop prints one JSON line whose `systemMessage` names the background task count and says the hop goes ahead at the first Stop after the tasks finish. It tells the operator to send a prompt or run `node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs cancel` to call the hop off. It carries no `decision` key, so Claude shows the notice and still ends the turn. When a record is armable and `payload.session_crons` is a non-empty array, Stop appends one `session-crons` line to `relaunch.log` with `loopId`, `sessionId` and `crons` set to the count. An empty, missing or non-array value writes nothing. A scheduled task and the todo list end with the session, so the receiver starts with neither. When the operator types a prompt in that session, the `UserPromptSubmit` nudge withdraws the session's record if it is deferred or already armed. The killer signals an armed session after a few quiet seconds, so a new turn that runs one long tool would otherwise be ended mid-turn and relaunched onto the old note. Claude, Codex, Pi, OpenCode and `cancel` a... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n137: \n138: Pi checks usage at `message_end` and holds the nudge, because the message that crosses the threshold usually carries the prepare call itself. At the next `turn_end` whose tool calls ran, those tools have finished, so it re-checks the pending or fresh armed record and the hop limit and steers the held text into the running loop with `deliverAs: 'steer'` and `triggerTurn: true`. The model reads it before its next call, as it reads a tool hook's context on Claude Code. A turn with no tool calls ends the loop, so `agent_end` makes the same checks and sends the text with `deliverAs: 'followUp'` instead. Either way the nudge reaches the model without an operator turn. Pi's advisor, goal and spec-gate extensions append their own blocks to the user's prompt after a blank line, so the seed handler reads the claim seed from the prompt's first block. Its adapter finds `killer.cjs` by walking up from any repository subdirectory. `tsconfig.pi.json` includes the adapter at its real path so it is type-checked. OpenCode adds its active nudge to the system prompt on every request while this session has no pending or fresh armed record. When `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` is set but names no record, it keeps the nudge only while the planned note file does not exist. It keeps a claim seed in the system prompt until the note leaves `open` or a new non-seed user message arrives, then arms the record on `session.idle`.\n139: \n140: Pi and OpenCode both withdraw an arm when the session they armed starts a new turn. The OpenCode killer runs without `--transcript`, so it only waits its idle time. The Pi killer watches the session file, but Pi writes an assistant message only once it completes, so a turn that is still thinking looks idle. A turn the operator starts just after the arm would otherwise be signalled mid-turn. OpenCode disarms on a new user message and Pi on `agent_start`, each through the shared withdraw helper and only for a record whose `sessionId` is its own. That record is an armed one, or a pending one an arm event could not arm: when arming throws at `session.idle` or `agent_settled`, the plugin logs `session-idle-arm-error` or `agent-settled-arm-error` with `stage=arm` and stamps `deferredAt` on the record. A pending record no arm event has reached yet is left alone. The record ends `disarmed` with reason `user-message`, the session gets the extra nudge, the hop history lists the note as withdrawn, the plugin logs `arm-cancelled`, and the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record` and stands down. Both then tell the model, with the same withdrawn notice Claude and Codex send, because a model that asked for the hand-over otherwise still expects to end and goes idle with nothing armed. Pi steers it into the run from `agent_start`, which Pi awaits before the run's first model call. OpenCode adds it to the system prompt of every request in that turn, until the next operator prompt or a compaction, whose stale-note text replaces it. The note stays open and the wrapper prints no claim command for it, because `should-relaunch` reports the record as `withdrawn`. OpenCode also drops the nudge it held, so the extra nudge replaces the old one instead of repeating it on the next turn. The OpenCode goal continuation is suppressed while a relaunch record for the session is pending or armed, because its idle turn would send the user message that withdraws the prepared hop.\n141: \n142: A question dialog holds the turn or run open until the operator answers, so a session that asks after `prepare` never reaches the event that arms its record. Claude Code, Pi and OpenCode therefore refuse a question dialog while the session's record can still be armed: a pending record, or an armed one with no live killer. Claude denies `AskUserQuestion` in `PreToolUse`, Pi blocks its `question` and `ask_user_question` tools in `tool_call`, and OpenCode throws from `tool.execute.before` for its `question` tool. All three take the reason from `questionHoldReason` in the shared nudge module, which names the note and the `cancel` command. Codex needs no guard: its question tool in a default session is `request_user_input_async`, which does not hold the turn, so the turn ends and the hop runs, and the unanswered question ends with the old session.\n143: \n144: The shared nudge module gives every adapter the note path and the same suppression rules. Outside `skilled-loop`, with `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` unset or empty, the nudge never asks for `prepare`, because prepare could only print a manual claim command and a session that stopped there would wait for a hand-off nobody runs. It asks for the note and `keep` instead, as Outside The Loop below describes. A re-nudge reuses the note this session already has open, matched on `session_id` and a `status` of `open`, and only takes a new name when no such note exists. A new note is named `<UTC stamp>-<session id>.md` with the full id, because OpenCode and Pi ids start with a shared time-based prefix and two sessions nudged in the same second would otherwise plan the same note. An open note under the older eight-character name is still found and reused. A record that is pending, or armed and still inside `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_AGE_MIN`, means the handoff steps already ran, so no adapter nudges. An armed record past the age limit is stale, cannot relaunch and no longer silences the nudge. The age limit resolves the way the wrapper resolves its switches, from the hook config file with the environment taking precedence. On Claude Code the nudge stays silent for a tool call that carries an `agent_id`, because a sub-agent or fork reports its parent's session and transcript, so its fill and its note would be the parent's.\n145: \n146: Inside the loop, no adapter nudges once `SKILLED_LOOP_HOPS` reaches `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_HOPS`, because a record prepared there could not relaunch. Every seed handler reads the note path back out of the seed, so the claim instructions it adds name the real note.\n147: \n148: Two hooks can decide on one nudge at once. Claude Code and Codex run a hook per tool call in a parallel batch, and a prompt hook beside a tool hook, and Vibe runs tool calls side by side. So the Claude, Codex and Vibe nudges deliver through `commitNudge` in the shared nudge module. Only the hook that creates the session's claim file in the state directory delivers, and it decides again under that claim before it writes the level, because a hook that delivered first has already written its own. A hook that finds the claim held stays quiet. A claim older than 10 seconds was left by a hook that died and is taken over.\n149: \n150: Codex reads the latest `token_count` record in the rollout named by the payload's `transcript_path` and nudges through `additionalContext` on `PostToolUse`. Its `UserPromptSubmit` entry point handles seed prompts and runs the same nudge check, because Codex binds each script to one event. Its `Stop` adapter accepts only `hook_event_name: Stop` and passes `transcript_path` to the killer. Codex runs its `SessionStart` hooks again with `source: compact` after it compacts a root session, before the next model request, so `codex/compact-pointer.mjs` resets the nudge level there and points the session at its open note, or sends the stale-note text for a withdrawn one, as Claude's pointer does. Claude and Codex write the armed state before starting the killer, so a killer spawn failure does not return the record to pending.\n151: \n152: Vibe reads fill from its journal and nudges from `turn-start` on `pre_agent` and from `compaction-post-tool` on every `post_tool`. Vibe runs tool calls side by side, so its nudge goes through the shared nudge claim described above. The `pre_agent` and `post_agent` hooks are registered for the `main` alias, which Vibe gives root sessions and forks, so a forked or rewound session keeps its hand-over. Only the unscoped `post_tool` path skips a payload with a `parent_session_id`. The first `turn-start` part withdraws an armed record before the slower parts run, and records the session id for the loop so `session-id --runtime vibe` can print it. `turn-end` on `post_agent` arms the record and hands the killer the session's whole `journal/` folder, because Vibe starts a new segment at the fold that ends the turn. The killer treats a folder as active while the folder or any numbered segment in it changes. A turn end that finds this session's pending record but cannot arm it marks the record deferred, so the next operator prompt withdraws it. After a compaction the Vibe part resets the nudge level, prints no claim pointer while a hop is pending, and sends the stale-note text for a withdrawn one.\n153: \n154: \n155: Every arm path restarts a killer for an armed record that has no live killer owner: Claude Stop, Codex Stop, OpenCode `session.idle` and Pi `agent_settled`. It does nothing while a killer owns the lock. A record left armed by a failed spawn is therefore recovered by the next arm event.\n156: \n157: The killer polls transcript mtime every 100 ms. When the transcript stays unchanged for `--idle-ms` (3000 ms by default), it confirms the target is a descendant of the loop and re-reads the record. It sends SIGTERM only when the record is still armed, below the hop cap and still fresh at the end of the SIGTERM grace period by the wrapper's own age rule. At or above the cap it unarms the record with reason `hop-cap`, logs `killer-hop-cap` and sends neither a signal nor a notification. A record that would be stale by then is unarmed with reason `stale` and logged as `killer-stale`, so the killer never ends a session the wrapper would refuse to relaunch. The OpenCode plugin runs on Bun, so it starts the killer with `node` instead of `process.execPath`, which points to the OpenCode CLI. It hands the killer the loop's direct child rather than its own pid, because OpenCode 2 loads plugins in a server its TUI starts, and signalling that server would leave the TUI running. Just before the signal the killer stamps `signalledAt` on the record, in the same locked step as its last check that the record is still the arm it watched, and a new arm drops the stamp. The `--loop-id` value must be greater than 1. If the record is no longer armed, the killer logs `killer-no-armed-record`. If the target exits during the SIGTERM call, it logs `target-gone`.\n158: \n159: The idle deadline (`--timeout-ms`, 30000 ms by default) does not shorten the 15000 ms grace period after SIGTERM, which leaves room for an OpenCode 2 TUI that was measured taking about six seconds to exit. If the idle deadline passes before the transcript is idle, or the target stays alive through the grace period, the killer disarms the record with reason `killer-timeout` and attempts a macOS notification with the manual claim command. Its title reads `Session handover needs attention (killer timed out)`, and when `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE` is unusable a subtitle says the template was ignored and gives the reason.\n160: \n161: A single failed transcript read is not a vanish. The killer gives up only when the transcript has stayed unreadable for 1 s without a break (`TRANSCRIPT_MISSING_GRACE_MS`). That give-up follows the timeout path: the record is disarmed with `killer-timeout` and the notification is attempted.\n162: \n163: ### Known Race: A Claude Goal Continuation During The Arm\n164: \n165: A Claude sender under an unmet native goal usually still hops, because no `UserPromptSubmit` fires for the continuation and the killer's 3 s idle wait lands in its first API call. The hop is lost with `killer-timeout` only when the continuation keeps writing less than 3 s apart for 30 s. A continuation tool call longer than 3 s can be killed mid-run, and that work is not in the note.\n166: \n167: The killer lock stores its owner pid, a creation timestamp and an expiry timestamp. The expiry is the idle timeout plus 15000 ms of signal wait, 3000 ms of notification time and a 60000 ms margin. A lock past its expiry, or a two-field lock from an earlier build that is older than 10 minutes, does not count as a live owner even while its pid exists, because that pid may by then belong to an unrelated process. A live owner or a lock younger than five seconds without a readable owner pid logs `killer-lock-held`. Taking over a stale lock first acquires a takeover mutex, the `<lock>.takeover` directory, whose `owner` file records the pid and token of the killer that holds it. The mutex is reclaimed only when that owner is dead. A mutex with no owner file is reclaimed once it is older than 30 s. The reclaim is an atomic rename. A live holder keeps exclusion only while the directory stays younger than the 10-minute hold bound, because a real holder holds the mutex for a re-check, an unlink and a create, never minutes. Past that bound the recorded pid counts as reused and the mutex is reclaimed. A killer that cannot acquire the mutex logs `killer-lock-held`. The mutex holder re-checks the lock, logs `killer-lock-stale`, removes the lock and retries once. Leftover `.stale-` takeover directories older than 30 s are swept under the mutex. The killer writes its pid and a random token to the mutex `owner` file. On release it renames the directory to a private `.stale-` name and removes it only when the inode it recorded and the token both still match, and otherwise renames it back. One window remains, and it needs three killers racing on the same loop's takeover: if a third killer recreates the mutex path while a mismatched directory is being renamed back, the displaced directory stays under its `.stale-` name until the stale sweep, and while it does, two killers can both pass the re-check, one can remove the lock the other just created with its exclusive open, and both then su... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n168: \n169: After the selected runtime exits, `skilled-loop` declines unless the record is armed, fresh and below the hop limit. It consumes an eligible record, increments the hop count and launches that runtime with a seed that carries the Gate 3 answer:\n170: \n171: ~~~text\n172: [session-handover] claim and continue handoff <note-path> | Spec folder: <spec-folder> (carried from the handoff; Gate 3 answered in the sending session)\n173: [session-handover] claim and continue handoff <note-path> | Spec folder: none (Gate 3 answered D, Skip, in the sending session)\n174: ~~~\n175: \n176: If the record is still pending when the runtime exits, `skilled-loop` prints the runtime-specific manual claim command on stdout and preserves the runtime's exit code. The core fills that command from `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE`. When a carried model, name, provider or Pi extension value contains the template's `@@HANDOFF@@` placeholder, the wrapper prints an error naming the value on stderr, then the plain command, and turns a clean runtime exit into exit 1. The CLI's `prepare` warns on stderr the same way when the template holds no placeholder or more than one. For a valid repo-relative `spec_folder`, the wrapper adds a `Spec folder:` suffix with the packet path. When the field is `none`, it adds a Skip suffix. An empty field adds no suffix, so the receiver asks Gate 3 as usual. A valid path carries the sending session's answer across the hop. AGENTS.md states that a relaunch hop is the same session. The receiver claims the note and reads its four sections. When the packet holds a `goal.md`, it reads that file before Remaining work. When In-flight state names an `Active command:` other than `none`, it runs that command again with the same mode and arguments. When the note names an `Active goal:` other than `none`, it calls `create_goal`, or tells the operator which goal to set on a runtime without it. For a packet path, it runs `/speckit:resume`. For `none`, it skips resume and treats Gate 3 as answered D) Skip. It works only on the task's Remaining work. It never runs `prepare` for the claimed note. It runs `complete` when the task work ends:\n177: \n178: ~~~bash\n179: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs claim <note-path> --session <new-session-id>\n180: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs complete <note-path>\n181: ~~~\n182: \n183: ### Goal Packet\n184: \n185: A note can carry an optional `goal_packet` frontmatter field: the repo-relative directory of the packet whose `goal.md` holds the sending session's goal. `prepare` and `keep` own the field. Each call resolves the sender's goal again and writes the result: the packet when the sender has a goal that can be proven, the packet goal of the note's spec folder for a Claude sender that never had a goal set, and no field at all otherwise. A value already in the note is replaced or removed, so a note prepared again after its goal ended stops handing the goal on, and a value written by hand does not survive. A Claude goal that ended also leaves a `goal_ended: true` field in the note, written and removed by the same two commands. Where the goal is found depends on the sender's runtime:\n186: \n187: | Sender | Where the goal is read |\n188: |---|---|\n189: | OpenCode, Pi, Devin, Cursor | The session's active goal record under `.skilled/skills/.state/goal/`, which names the bound packet. A paused, completed or cleared goal counts as none. Devin and Cursor have no record under the fresh native id a manual claim starts, so they also keep the packet the claim exported in `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` when it names a real packet. |\n190: | Vibe | No goal record. The packet the Vibe goal hook renders from: `VIBE_SPEC_FOLDER` when it is set, otherwise the `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` a claim exported, either one only when it names a real packet. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 191]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \".skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794808040261,"end":1794808040435}}}}
