{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794808059284,"sessionID":"ses_e1d1c612bffenqRaMYnBfMfTGv","part":{"partID":"prt_1e2e593a4002iRq5dBpvD2s1PT","sessionID":"ses_e1d1c612bffenqRaMYnBfMfTGv","messageID":"msg_1e2e5823e0017lI4GLnwKuH4vi","type":"tool","id":"call_00_wsh43rj4pxybk7ut107ecjdt","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":120,"offset":191,"path":".skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md, lines 191-310\n191: | Claude | The newest goal entry in the session log. A goal that is met, failed or cleared counts as none. The packet is the spec folder, one of its parent packets or a packet the goal text names, and its `goal.md` must carry the goal's opening line. When the spec folder and the named packets all miss the line, a packet under `specs/` whose `goal.md` carries it is used if exactly one does. With no readable log no packet can be proven, and a mark the note this session claimed last carries still stands. A log that holds no goal entry at all means no goal was ever set in that session. The field then names the nearest packet, the spec folder first and then its parent and no packet further up, whose `goal.md` still has an unticked completion criterion and a slice within the budget, so the receiver starts on that goal without the operator typing it. A leaf phase goal has no slice budget and gives way to its parent. A finished `goal.md` is skipped. Past the 16 MB cap, a tail that holds no goal entry is followed by a read of the bytes before it in fixed chunks, so the newest entry anywhere in the log decides, and a log with no entry at all reaches the packet goal fallback. A goal that is met, failed or cleared is marked in the note as `goal_ended: true`. A receiver that sets no goal has a log with no goal entry, so its own hand-over reads the mark from the note it claimed last, found beside the note it hands over, carries no packet goal and writes the mark onward. A goal set in a later session is carried as usual and takes the mark away. |\n192: | Codex | Nowhere, so no field is written. Codex keeps a goal per thread in `goals_1.sqlite` under its home, with the objective text and a status and no packet, and no populated record has been read from it. A spec folder that holds a `goal.md` does not show that the sender had a goal, so a Codex hand-over carries no goal packet. A Codex sender names its goal on the note's `Active goal:` line instead, because Codex still has no goal reader. |\n193: \n194: A written value is checked the way `spec_folder` is: a repo-relative packet path whose directory and `goal.md` stay inside the repository once links are followed, in this checkout or in the working directory the note names. A resolver that finds nothing, cannot prove the goal or cannot read its source leaves the field out and never fails the hand-over. The note carries the path and never the goal text, because a copy would go stale while `goal.md` changes. When no packet is carried and the resolver has a reason, `prepare` and `keep` print one `[session-handover] goal not carried: <reason>` line on stderr and leave the field out. A note written before this field claims and hands over unchanged.\n195: \n196: `claim` prints the note body, then the `complete` command that closes the note, because some runtimes see the claim instructions only once and a model that answers straight from the body can skip that step. It ends its output with a `Goal packet: <path>` line when the packet still holds a `goal.md`. `consume` reports the packet as `goal_packet`, and `skilled-loop` exports it as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` to the relaunched session. The wrapper removes any value an outer session left from the first launch, and from any hop whose note carries no packet. A first launch started by the printed manual claim command is the one first launch that gets a packet: the wrapper finds the claim seed among the operator's arguments by its prefix, reads the note it names and, only when that note is open, its real path is inside the handoffs directory and its packet passes the same checks `consume` applies, exports the packet the way a hop does. On Claude it replaces that one argument with `/goal <slice>` and moves the seed to `SKILLED_LOOP_CLAIM_SEED`. Every other operator argument stays where it was. Binding the goal in the receiving runtime is the receiving adapter's work: this layer records the packet and passes it on, and the wrapper itself acts for Claude as described below. `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL=0` turns all of it off. `prepare` and `keep` then neither write, replace nor remove the field, and `claim` and the wrapper pass nothing on. A relaunch follows the switch as it stood when `prepare` wrote the record: a note prepared with the switch off hands on no packet even when the switch is on again by the time the record is consumed. A manual `claim` has no record to read, so it follows the switch at claim time. Pi and OpenCode bind a hop goal once per process and delete `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` after the attempt, so a later session or a nested runtime cannot bind it.\n197: \n198: ### History Lookup After A Claim\n199: \n200: A handoff note holds what the sender chose to write down. The history lookup gives the receiver a way to read the sender's own earlier turns for a detail the note left out, such as an exact code, a path or a figure, instead of guessing it.\n201: \n202: **What the claim prints.** `claim` mints a history locator for the sending session with provenance source `handover` and prints a `## History Lookup` section after the note body, then the sentence `Use it for a detail this note does not spell out, before you guess.` The section names one read-only command, `node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/history-lookup.mjs --session <id> --handle <hl1_...> <operation> <json>`, which searches, browses, loads and pages the sender's earlier turns. The command runs in a subshell that first moves to the checkout root, so it works from any subfolder. A locator from any sender other than Claude Code adds its runtime in front of it, such as `SPECKIT_RUNTIME=codex` or `SPECKIT_RUNTIME=pi`.\n203: \n204: **When the section is printed.** The claim mints a locator and prints the section only when all of these hold:\n205: \n206: 1. The sender ran Claude Code, Codex, Vibe, Pi or OpenCode. The other runtimes have no handover locator. Pi and OpenCode keep no transcript file a handle could name, so the claim asks the same session reader the lookup uses, with the folder the note names, and mints only when that reader finds the session there. A session the store records for another folder is refused, as the lookup would refuse it.\n207: 2. The sender's transcript exists in the runtime's own store. The claim finds it by session id and never takes a path from the note.\n208: 3. The note's folder sits in the receiver's checkout, and so does the first folder the transcript records. This stops a note from pointing the lookup at another project's session.\n209: 4. That checkout root holds the runnable entry and the engine's build. Without them the printed command could not run.\n210: 5. The locator write persisted. A locator the state schema refuses, for example a session id too long to print, prints nothing.\n211: \n212: When any condition fails, the claim prints exactly what it printed before the lookup existed. A failed mint never fails the claim.\n213: \n214: **How the locator is proven.** The locator is saved in the sender's hook state, keyed by the checkout root. Its handle, `hl1_` plus 32 hex characters, is a digest of the locator's fields and the transcript's path, so a model cannot write a handle that works. On every call the engine re-reads the saved locator and checks the session, the handle, the runtime and the working folder against it. It then finds the transcript and re-derives the digest from it before it answers.\n215: \n216: **The 30-minute window.** A locator is fresh for 30 minutes from the claim. A note may be claimed hours after it was prepared, so the claim mints a fresh locator every time rather than reusing an old one. A timestamp in the future counts as clock skew and is stale.\n217: \n218: **When the command refuses.** Every refusal prints one `{\"ok\":false,\"code\":\"<code>\"}` line and exits 2. The codes a receiver is most likely to meet:\n219: \n220: - `locator_stale`: more than 30 minutes passed since the claim.\n221: - `workspace_mismatch`: the command ran outside the checkout that minted the locator.\n222: - `store_unreadable`: the runtime's transcript store is missing or could not be read.\n223: - `core_unavailable`: the entry could not load the engine's build, so the engine needs a build, as section 7 shows.\n224: \n225: The history engine's README lists all 22 codes.\n226: \n227: **Why the claim is the only place that offers it.** The lookup used to be offered after a compaction, and the locator used to be minted at `prepare`. Both are gone:\n228: \n229: - Receivers never ran the lookup when only a compaction offered it: 0 of 6 counted runs tried it, and none recovered the detail. With the section printed at the claim, 6 of 6 ran it.\n230: - A locator minted at `prepare` is often stale by the time the note is claimed, and the claim mints a fresh one anyway. The operator chose to drop the `prepare` mint for that reason.\n231: \n232: **What the measurement shows, and its limits.** In a hop-recall harness the sender saw a code the note did not hold, and the receiver was asked for it. Before the change, 0 of 6 receivers recovered it. After the claim offer, and after a fix to the shared Claude transcript reader, 3 of 3 answered correctly. That is six counted runs per side, on one runtime, one model and one kind of detail. A wider measurement on Claude Code, Codex and Vibe is planned, not yet run.\n233: \n234: **Where the engine lives.** The engine is the [`history/`](history/README.md) package in this folder: TypeScript source in `history/lib/`, its build in `history/dist/` and its suites in `history/tests/`. It reads the hook state that the spec-kit runtime compiles, so the runtime builds first. Its README covers the four operations, the reply budgets, the search rules and every refusal code. Search matches whole identifier parts, split on `-`, `.`, `/`, `:` and `_` and on case changes. A query matches whole word parts, so 'live' does not match 'delivery'.\n235: \n236: ### Goal In A Claude Receiver\n237: \n238: Claude has no goal record a hook can bind, so a Claude receiver gets its goal as the start prompt: `/goal <slice>`. The slice is read from `goal.md` at launch with `goal.cjs packet <packet> --workspace <root>`, so it is the file as it is now and never a copy. Only the `chat_slice` is sent, with no frontmatter, comments or anchors, and only when the command reports `packet_budget=ok` and the slice fits 4,000 characters, the limit Claude puts on a goal condition. A start prompt can be the command and nothing else, and a start hook cannot send the command in an interactive session, so the claim seed moves out of the prompt. `skilled-loop` exports it as `SKILLED_LOOP_CLAIM_SEED`, and the `SessionStart` hook in `claude/compact-pointer.mjs` delivers the claim instructions as start context, with the seed's spec folder and Gate 3 answer, before any goal work. The hook acts only on the `startup` source and delivers the claim even while the Claude enable switch is off, because the wrapper already swapped the start prompt and the start context is the claim's only route. The master hook switch and the concern kill switch silence the whole hook. `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL=0`, a dispatched child (`AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`) and a runtime started from the loop session's shell keep the claim silent, the last because the variable carries the `SKILLED_LOOP_` prefix the nested-run check strips. In the live runs of 2026-11-13, a Claude receiver claimed its note in ten of ten counted hops under a goal that allows other work and in two of five under a test goal whose text tells the session to do nothing else. The claim reaches the receiver as start context only. A claim line in the start prompt was declined, because every counted receiver under the first goal claimed without one. A goal whose text forbids other work can make a receiver skip its claim.\n239: \n240: When no slice can be sent, the receiver keeps the claim seed as its start prompt and the hop works as it did before this section. The wrapper prints one `warning: the goal was not set in the relaunched session: <reason>` line when a packet was named and the slice is refused: the packet lost its `goal.md`, the slice is over the limit, the repository has no budget to check it against or `goal.md` has no durable slice. A hop with the switch off or no packet prints nothing. A Claude receiver is a new session with no goal, so nothing replaces an existing one.\n241: \n242: A phase child carries no durable-slice budget, so the goal command reports `packet_budget=unknown` and Claude keeps the claim seed and sets no goal, because it sets a goal only from a slice whose budget is `ok`. Pi and OpenCode bind the packet itself and do bind a phase-child goal.\n243: \n244: \n245: Codex gets no goal packet of its own. Its start prompt cannot be the goal command, and a hop always relaunches the runtime it left, so a Codex hop never carries a packet. Codex still has no goal reader, so a Codex sender names its goal on the note's `Active goal:` line instead. A manual claim can still carry a packet: when Codex starts with the claim seed of a note another runtime wrote, the wrapper exports that note's packet as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET`, and `claim` ends with its `Goal packet:` line. The seed instruction tells the receiver to call `create_goal` when the note's `Active goal:` line names one, and to tell the operator which goal to set on a runtime without `create_goal`.\n246: \n247: ### Goal Carry-Over: What Was Shown Live\n248: \n249: Live hops on 2026-10-25, each in a throwaway checkout with a private tmux server and runtime directory, read the receiver and not the sender:\n250: \n251: - **Claude and Pi:** a sender with a goal handed it on, the receiver held it, and the two controls (`SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL=0`, and a sender with no goal) left the receiver with none. On Claude the receiver's log shows a goal of 3,990 characters and a start claim. On Pi it shows a goal block with a new goal id.\n252: - **Codex:** a hop carries no packet by design. The control shows a note with no `goal_packet` line and an empty `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` in the receiver.\n253: - **OpenCode 2:** a hop with a goal reached the receiver on OpenCode 2.1.4 started with `--standalone`: the receiver claimed and completed the note, bound the packet as a new goal record and saw it in `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET`. The receiver also binds a packet it is given and stays empty under the switch and with no packet. On its default background service the loop does not run at all, because the plugin runs in a service no wrapper started and that service holds no `SKILLED_LOOP_ID`, so nothing arms. The wrapper adds `--standalone` to the first launch and to every relaunch when `opencode --help` lists the flag, so the operator does not pass it. A launch that carries `--server` stays on that server, prints a warning and never arms. Running `opencode mini` or another subcommand skips the wrapper.\n254: - **Devin and Cursor:** both ship on the command trigger, so nothing arms a prepared record and the wrapper never relaunches them. A manual start by the printed command goes through the first-launch path, which exports the packet of the note its seed names as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET`. No hop ever hands them a packet. The Devin receiver half bound a packet in a headless session with the variable set by hand. Cursor could not run: it was not logged in.\n255: \n256: The playbooks hold the repeatable form of each run: the Goal Carry-Over checks in the Claude, Pi and OpenCode, and command-runtime relaunch scenarios.\n257: \n258: ### Outside The Loop\n259: \n260: A session started without `skilled-loop`, such as one opened before the shell shim was sourced, cannot relaunch. Auto-compaction then shortens its context to a summary, and the summary drops detail: merge decisions, open conflicts and exact next steps. At the threshold the nudge therefore says the session cannot relaunch and asks it to write the same note, run `/speckit:save` when a packet is bound, and then run `keep`:\n261: \n262: ~~~bash\n263: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs keep --handoff <note-path> --session <session-id> --runtime <runtime>\n264: ~~~\n265: \n266: `keep` validates the note the way `prepare` does, closes this session's earlier claimed notes, appends a `note-kept` line to the hop history, prints the `claim` and `complete` commands and exits 0. The session keeps working. While this session has an open note, the nudge stays quiet, so writing the note does not set off the reminder 0.05 higher. Inside the loop `keep` refuses with exit 2 and names `prepare`. On Claude Code, the `compact-pointer` hook on `SessionStart` reads the event's `source`. After a compaction it resets this session's nudge level, so the next fill nudges again, and adds the claim instructions for the session's open note, so the compacted session claims the note, works its Remaining work and completes it. It adds nothing while a prepared hop owns the note. When the record for that note was withdrawn or cancelled and the session has not written to the note since, the hook logs `compact-pointer-stale-note` and tells the session to read the note for background only and not to claim it. The note predates the work done since the withdraw, so the session updates it at the next nudge. Pi does the same on `session_compact` and OpenCode on `session.compacted`: each resets the session's nudge level, drops a nudge it still holds, because that text quotes the fill the compaction freed, and sends the claim instructions for the open note, with Pi's sent as a hidden message and OpenCode's added to the next system prompt. Each logs `compact-pointer`. When the record for the note was withdrawn and the session has not written to the note since, Pi and OpenCode send the same read-only note text Claude does and log `compact-pointer-stale-note` instead. OpenCode keeps that text in the system prompt only while the withdrawal stands. Codex has no compaction hook here, so their sessions follow the claim command `keep` printed. Before it resets the nudge level or points at a note, the hook skips when the event carries no `transcript_path`, when the transcript file is not... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n267: \n268: ### Sub-Agents And Nested Runs\n269: \n270: An OpenCode `task` sub-agent is a child session with its own id. The plugin records every session whose `session.created` or `session.updated` event carries `info.parentID` and skips it everywhere, so a child never gets a nudge, a seed or an arm. The plugin tracks each child's `parentID` and a `busy` flag in `childSessions`, and a child stays `busy` from its creation until its `session.idle` or `session.deleted` event. A parent `session.idle` with a busy child is held in `deferredArms` and arms nothing, and the child that ends last runs the deferred arm for its parent. The plugin sets the session title from `SKILLED_LOOP_NAME` on a claim seed through `ctx.client.session.update`, and the call is not awaited, so an absent or failing `update` still leaves the seed in the system prompt. Pi has no built-in sub-agent.\n271: \n272: A worker an orchestrator dispatches through a CLI is marked with `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`. On every adapter, the nudge decision returns `child-session` for it at any fill, so a worker never gets told to save continuity or write a handoff into its parent's packet. The claim path skips such a child the same way.\n273: \n274: A runtime started from inside a looped session, such as `claude -p` or `pi -p` run from a tool shell, inherits `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` without being the loop's runtime. The Claude, Pi and OpenCode adapters and the `prepare`, `keep` and `cancel` commands check the process tree first (the Codex adapters do not): when two or more processes sit between the host runtime and the loop pid, the run is nested. The host is the nearest ancestor that is neither a shell nor an OpenCode process whose parent is also an OpenCode process. OpenCode 2 runs each tool shell under a pty daemon that its serve process starts, and counting those two would read the loop's own OpenCode session as nested. A nested run logs `loop-nested-run` and drops every `SKILLED_LOOP_` variable from its own environment. That includes `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE` and the carried model, name and provider values, which would otherwise make the nested run print the outer loop's command. A nested run then behaves as a session outside the loop, and `prepare` there exits 3 instead of arming the outer loop. `cancel` drops the inherited loop id the same way, so it never cancels the outer hand-over unless you pass `--loop-id`. The check fails open, so a process table it cannot read leaves the variables in place.\n275: \n276: ---\n277: \n278: ## 4. STATE FILES\n279: \n280: The relaunch record uses `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/skilled-relaunch` when `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is set. When it is unset, the record uses `skilled-relaunch-<uid>` under the operating system temporary directory, because a shared temp directory would otherwise let the first user on a host create a directory the second user cannot own. The directory must be a real directory owned by the current user. A symlink, a plain file or a foreign owner fails with `relaunch state directory must be a real directory owned by the current user: <dir>`, and a directory that passes is set to mode 0700. Its filename is `relaunch-<loop-id>.json`.\n281: \n282: | Relaunch status | Meaning |\n283: |---|---|\n284: | pending | Prepare accepted the note. The runtime's arm event has not armed the record yet. |\n285: | armed | The runtime's arm event armed the record and started the killer: `Stop` on Claude and Codex, `agent_settled` on Pi, and `session.idle` on OpenCode. |\n286: | disarmed | The record ended without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and one past its age limit ends with reason `stale`. In both cases the killer sends no signal. A new turn in the arming session ends an armed or deferred record with reason `user-message` on Pi and OpenCode, and so does an operator prompt over an armed or deferred record on Claude and Codex. `cancel` ends a pending or armed record with reason `cancelled`. `should-relaunch` reports both of those reasons as `withdrawn` and every other record that is not armed as `not-armed`. |\n287: \n288: A corrupt relaunch file is treated as absent. `should-relaunch` declines it with reason `no-file` and logs `corrupt-relaunch-file`. A record that parses but holds no `handoff` string is read the same way, because no writer produces one and a pending record without a note would let Stop arm a hop that has nothing to claim. A record whose `handoff` is an empty string is still read, and `should-relaunch` declines it as `incomplete-record`. The CLI also treats `EACCES`, `ENOTDIR` and `EISDIR` while reading the record as absent. Other errors return exit 2.\n289: \n290: The handoff note has a separate status field:\n291: \n292: | Note status | Meaning |\n293: |---|---|\n294: | open | Written and ready for a receiving session. |\n295: | claimed | The receiving session claimed it. The note records `claimed_by` and `claimed_at`. |\n296: | done | The receiving session completed the handoff, or `prepare` closed an earlier note of this session when it prepared a successor hop. |\n297: \n298: When a new hop goes ahead, `prepare` marks as `done` every other note this session claimed and every other open note this session wrote, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n299: \n300: The hook keeps per-session nudge levels in `nudge-<session-id>.json` in the same runtime state directory. After a withdraw or a `cancel` the file also holds the one extra nudge as a `rearm` field. The field starts pending and becomes a fill floor, the fill at the first check at or past the threshold plus 0.05, without nudging on that check. A delivered nudge or a compaction writes the level alone and so spends the grant. Every write to a relaunch record (prepare, arm, disarm, defer, withdraw and the removal `consume` makes) and the claim and completion of a note run under a lock beside the file, so two writers cannot both act on the same read. A lock that cannot be taken lets the write go ahead without it. The hooks and wrapper append events to `relaunch.log` on a best-effort basis. The killer appends its events there too. A failed append in the killer is not swallowed. Each event is one line, appended under fsync at mode 0600, with the mode re-pinned on every append. When the previous last line has no newline, as after a partial write, the new event starts on its own line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A short write, from a full disk or a file-size limit, throws `relaunch log short write: wrote X of Y bytes to <path>` instead of losing the event quietly. The killer lock file stores the owner pid, the creation timestamp and the expiry stamp. A lock past its expiry counts as stale, so a reused pid cannot keep the loop pinned. Active or newly created locks log `killer-lock-held`. Stale locks log `killer-lock-stale` before the killer removes and retries them.\n301: \n302: ### Hop History\n303: \n304: `relaunch.log` sits in a temporary directory the system clears, so it is no record of past hops. Each hop therefore also appends one line to `.skilled/skills/.state/session-handover/hop-history.log`, beside the handoff notes. That folder is gitignored and persists. A repository that links this checkout's `.skilled` writes to the same file, and each line carries the session's folder so you can tell them apart.\n305: \n306: | Event | Written when |\n307: |---|---|\n308: | `handed-over` | The wrapper passed its last checks and relaunched, so the session handed over. `should-relaunch` writes nothing, because the wrapper can still decline after it approves. The line carries the hop number. |\n309: | `claimed` | A session claimed the note. The line ends with the first eight characters of the claiming session's id, and `history` prints them as `by <id>`. The note's file name names the session that wrote it, so a session that claimed its own note shows the same id in both. |\n310: | `done` | The fresh session completed the note. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 311]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md, lines 191-310\n191: | Claude | The newest goal entry in the session log. A goal that is met, failed or cleared counts as none. The packet is the spec folder, one of its parent packets or a packet the goal text names, and its `goal.md` must carry the goal's opening line. When the spec folder and the named packets all miss the line, a packet under `specs/` whose `goal.md` carries it is used if exactly one does. With no readable log no packet can be proven, and a mark the note this session claimed last carries still stands. A log that holds no goal entry at all means no goal was ever set in that session. The field then names the nearest packet, the spec folder first and then its parent and no packet further up, whose `goal.md` still has an unticked completion criterion and a slice within the budget, so the receiver starts on that goal without the operator typing it. A leaf phase goal has no slice budget and gives way to its parent. A finished `goal.md` is skipped. Past the 16 MB cap, a tail that holds no goal entry is followed by a read of the bytes before it in fixed chunks, so the newest entry anywhere in the log decides, and a log with no entry at all reaches the packet goal fallback. A goal that is met, failed or cleared is marked in the note as `goal_ended: true`. A receiver that sets no goal has a log with no goal entry, so its own hand-over reads the mark from the note it claimed last, found beside the note it hands over, carries no packet goal and writes the mark onward. A goal set in a later session is carried as usual and takes the mark away. |\n192: | Codex | Nowhere, so no field is written. Codex keeps a goal per thread in `goals_1.sqlite` under its home, with the objective text and a status and no packet, and no populated record has been read from it. A spec folder that holds a `goal.md` does not show that the sender had a goal, so a Codex hand-over carries no goal packet. A Codex sender names its goal on the note's `Active goal:` line instead, because Codex still has no goal reader. |\n193: \n194: A written value is checked the way `spec_folder` is: a repo-relative packet path whose directory and `goal.md` stay inside the repository once links are followed, in this checkout or in the working directory the note names. A resolver that finds nothing, cannot prove the goal or cannot read its source leaves the field out and never fails the hand-over. The note carries the path and never the goal text, because a copy would go stale while `goal.md` changes. When no packet is carried and the resolver has a reason, `prepare` and `keep` print one `[session-handover] goal not carried: <reason>` line on stderr and leave the field out. A note written before this field claims and hands over unchanged.\n195: \n196: `claim` prints the note body, then the `complete` command that closes the note, because some runtimes see the claim instructions only once and a model that answers straight from the body can skip that step. It ends its output with a `Goal packet: <path>` line when the packet still holds a `goal.md`. `consume` reports the packet as `goal_packet`, and `skilled-loop` exports it as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` to the relaunched session. The wrapper removes any value an outer session left from the first launch, and from any hop whose note carries no packet. A first launch started by the printed manual claim command is the one first launch that gets a packet: the wrapper finds the claim seed among the operator's arguments by its prefix, reads the note it names and, only when that note is open, its real path is inside the handoffs directory and its packet passes the same checks `consume` applies, exports the packet the way a hop does. On Claude it replaces that one argument with `/goal <slice>` and moves the seed to `SKILLED_LOOP_CLAIM_SEED`. Every other operator argument stays where it was. Binding the goal in the receiving runtime is the receiving adapter's work: this layer records the packet and passes it on, and the wrapper itself acts for Claude as described below. `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL=0` turns all of it off. `prepare` and `keep` then neither write, replace nor remove the field, and `claim` and the wrapper pass nothing on. A relaunch follows the switch as it stood when `prepare` wrote the record: a note prepared with the switch off hands on no packet even when the switch is on again by the time the record is consumed. A manual `claim` has no record to read, so it follows the switch at claim time. Pi and OpenCode bind a hop goal once per process and delete `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` after the attempt, so a later session or a nested runtime cannot bind it.\n197: \n198: ### History Lookup After A Claim\n199: \n200: A handoff note holds what the sender chose to write down. The history lookup gives the receiver a way to read the sender's own earlier turns for a detail the note left out, such as an exact code, a path or a figure, instead of guessing it.\n201: \n202: **What the claim prints.** `claim` mints a history locator for the sending session with provenance source `handover` and prints a `## History Lookup` section after the note body, then the sentence `Use it for a detail this note does not spell out, before you guess.` The section names one read-only command, `node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/history-lookup.mjs --session <id> --handle <hl1_...> <operation> <json>`, which searches, browses, loads and pages the sender's earlier turns. The command runs in a subshell that first moves to the checkout root, so it works from any subfolder. A locator from any sender other than Claude Code adds its runtime in front of it, such as `SPECKIT_RUNTIME=codex` or `SPECKIT_RUNTIME=pi`.\n203: \n204: **When the section is printed.** The claim mints a locator and prints the section only when all of these hold:\n205: \n206: 1. The sender ran Claude Code, Codex, Vibe, Pi or OpenCode. The other runtimes have no handover locator. Pi and OpenCode keep no transcript file a handle could name, so the claim asks the same session reader the lookup uses, with the folder the note names, and mints only when that reader finds the session there. A session the store records for another folder is refused, as the lookup would refuse it.\n207: 2. The sender's transcript exists in the runtime's own store. The claim finds it by session id and never takes a path from the note.\n208: 3. The note's folder sits in the receiver's checkout, and so does the first folder the transcript records. This stops a note from pointing the lookup at another project's session.\n209: 4. That checkout root holds the runnable entry and the engine's build. Without them the printed command could not run.\n210: 5. The locator write persisted. A locator the state schema refuses, for example a session id too long to print, prints nothing.\n211: \n212: When any condition fails, the claim prints exactly what it printed before the lookup existed. A failed mint never fails the claim.\n213: \n214: **How the locator is proven.** The locator is saved in the sender's hook state, keyed by the checkout root. Its handle, `hl1_` plus 32 hex characters, is a digest of the locator's fields and the transcript's path, so a model cannot write a handle that works. On every call the engine re-reads the saved locator and checks the session, the handle, the runtime and the working folder against it. It then finds the transcript and re-derives the digest from it before it answers.\n215: \n216: **The 30-minute window.** A locator is fresh for 30 minutes from the claim. A note may be claimed hours after it was prepared, so the claim mints a fresh locator every time rather than reusing an old one. A timestamp in the future counts as clock skew and is stale.\n217: \n218: **When the command refuses.** Every refusal prints one `{\"ok\":false,\"code\":\"<code>\"}` line and exits 2. The codes a receiver is most likely to meet:\n219: \n220: - `locator_stale`: more than 30 minutes passed since the claim.\n221: - `workspace_mismatch`: the command ran outside the checkout that minted the locator.\n222: - `store_unreadable`: the runtime's transcript store is missing or could not be read.\n223: - `core_unavailable`: the entry could not load the engine's build, so the engine needs a build, as section 7 shows.\n224: \n225: The history engine's README lists all 22 codes.\n226: \n227: **Why the claim is the only place that offers it.** The lookup used to be offered after a compaction, and the locator used to be minted at `prepare`. Both are gone:\n228: \n229: - Receivers never ran the lookup when only a compaction offered it: 0 of 6 counted runs tried it, and none recovered the detail. With the section printed at the claim, 6 of 6 ran it.\n230: - A locator minted at `prepare` is often stale by the time the note is claimed, and the claim mints a fresh one anyway. The operator chose to drop the `prepare` mint for that reason.\n231: \n232: **What the measurement shows, and its limits.** In a hop-recall harness the sender saw a code the note did not hold, and the receiver was asked for it. Before the change, 0 of 6 receivers recovered it. After the claim offer, and after a fix to the shared Claude transcript reader, 3 of 3 answered correctly. That is six counted runs per side, on one runtime, one model and one kind of detail. A wider measurement on Claude Code, Codex and Vibe is planned, not yet run.\n233: \n234: **Where the engine lives.** The engine is the [`history/`](history/README.md) package in this folder: TypeScript source in `history/lib/`, its build in `history/dist/` and its suites in `history/tests/`. It reads the hook state that the spec-kit runtime compiles, so the runtime builds first. Its README covers the four operations, the reply budgets, the search rules and every refusal code. Search matches whole identifier parts, split on `-`, `.`, `/`, `:` and `_` and on case changes. A query matches whole word parts, so 'live' does not match 'delivery'.\n235: \n236: ### Goal In A Claude Receiver\n237: \n238: Claude has no goal record a hook can bind, so a Claude receiver gets its goal as the start prompt: `/goal <slice>`. The slice is read from `goal.md` at launch with `goal.cjs packet <packet> --workspace <root>`, so it is the file as it is now and never a copy. Only the `chat_slice` is sent, with no frontmatter, comments or anchors, and only when the command reports `packet_budget=ok` and the slice fits 4,000 characters, the limit Claude puts on a goal condition. A start prompt can be the command and nothing else, and a start hook cannot send the command in an interactive session, so the claim seed moves out of the prompt. `skilled-loop` exports it as `SKILLED_LOOP_CLAIM_SEED`, and the `SessionStart` hook in `claude/compact-pointer.mjs` delivers the claim instructions as start context, with the seed's spec folder and Gate 3 answer, before any goal work. The hook acts only on the `startup` source and delivers the claim even while the Claude enable switch is off, because the wrapper already swapped the start prompt and the start context is the claim's only route. The master hook switch and the concern kill switch silence the whole hook. `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL=0`, a dispatched child (`AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`) and a runtime started from the loop session's shell keep the claim silent, the last because the variable carries the `SKILLED_LOOP_` prefix the nested-run check strips. In the live runs of 2026-11-13, a Claude receiver claimed its note in ten of ten counted hops under a goal that allows other work and in two of five under a test goal whose text tells the session to do nothing else. The claim reaches the receiver as start context only. A claim line in the start prompt was declined, because every counted receiver under the first goal claimed without one. A goal whose text forbids other work can make a receiver skip its claim.\n239: \n240: When no slice can be sent, the receiver keeps the claim seed as its start prompt and the hop works as it did before this section. The wrapper prints one `warning: the goal was not set in the relaunched session: <reason>` line when a packet was named and the slice is refused: the packet lost its `goal.md`, the slice is over the limit, the repository has no budget to check it against or `goal.md` has no durable slice. A hop with the switch off or no packet prints nothing. A Claude receiver is a new session with no goal, so nothing replaces an existing one.\n241: \n242: A phase child carries no durable-slice budget, so the goal command reports `packet_budget=unknown` and Claude keeps the claim seed and sets no goal, because it sets a goal only from a slice whose budget is `ok`. Pi and OpenCode bind the packet itself and do bind a phase-child goal.\n243: \n244: \n245: Codex gets no goal packet of its own. Its start prompt cannot be the goal command, and a hop always relaunches the runtime it left, so a Codex hop never carries a packet. Codex still has no goal reader, so a Codex sender names its goal on the note's `Active goal:` line instead. A manual claim can still carry a packet: when Codex starts with the claim seed of a note another runtime wrote, the wrapper exports that note's packet as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET`, and `claim` ends with its `Goal packet:` line. The seed instruction tells the receiver to call `create_goal` when the note's `Active goal:` line names one, and to tell the operator which goal to set on a runtime without `create_goal`.\n246: \n247: ### Goal Carry-Over: What Was Shown Live\n248: \n249: Live hops on 2026-10-25, each in a throwaway checkout with a private tmux server and runtime directory, read the receiver and not the sender:\n250: \n251: - **Claude and Pi:** a sender with a goal handed it on, the receiver held it, and the two controls (`SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL=0`, and a sender with no goal) left the receiver with none. On Claude the receiver's log shows a goal of 3,990 characters and a start claim. On Pi it shows a goal block with a new goal id.\n252: - **Codex:** a hop carries no packet by design. The control shows a note with no `goal_packet` line and an empty `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET` in the receiver.\n253: - **OpenCode 2:** a hop with a goal reached the receiver on OpenCode 2.1.4 started with `--standalone`: the receiver claimed and completed the note, bound the packet as a new goal record and saw it in `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET`. The receiver also binds a packet it is given and stays empty under the switch and with no packet. On its default background service the loop does not run at all, because the plugin runs in a service no wrapper started and that service holds no `SKILLED_LOOP_ID`, so nothing arms. The wrapper adds `--standalone` to the first launch and to every relaunch when `opencode --help` lists the flag, so the operator does not pass it. A launch that carries `--server` stays on that server, prints a warning and never arms. Running `opencode mini` or another subcommand skips the wrapper.\n254: - **Devin and Cursor:** both ship on the command trigger, so nothing arms a prepared record and the wrapper never relaunches them. A manual start by the printed command goes through the first-launch path, which exports the packet of the note its seed names as `SESSION_HANDOVER_GOAL_PACKET`. No hop ever hands them a packet. The Devin receiver half bound a packet in a headless session with the variable set by hand. Cursor could not run: it was not logged in.\n255: \n256: The playbooks hold the repeatable form of each run: the Goal Carry-Over checks in the Claude, Pi and OpenCode, and command-runtime relaunch scenarios.\n257: \n258: ### Outside The Loop\n259: \n260: A session started without `skilled-loop`, such as one opened before the shell shim was sourced, cannot relaunch. Auto-compaction then shortens its context to a summary, and the summary drops detail: merge decisions, open conflicts and exact next steps. At the threshold the nudge therefore says the session cannot relaunch and asks it to write the same note, run `/speckit:save` when a packet is bound, and then run `keep`:\n261: \n262: ~~~bash\n263: node .skilled/hooks/session-handover/bin/session-handover.cjs keep --handoff <note-path> --session <session-id> --runtime <runtime>\n264: ~~~\n265: \n266: `keep` validates the note the way `prepare` does, closes this session's earlier claimed notes, appends a `note-kept` line to the hop history, prints the `claim` and `complete` commands and exits 0. The session keeps working. While this session has an open note, the nudge stays quiet, so writing the note does not set off the reminder 0.05 higher. Inside the loop `keep` refuses with exit 2 and names `prepare`. On Claude Code, the `compact-pointer` hook on `SessionStart` reads the event's `source`. After a compaction it resets this session's nudge level, so the next fill nudges again, and adds the claim instructions for the session's open note, so the compacted session claims the note, works its Remaining work and completes it. It adds nothing while a prepared hop owns the note. When the record for that note was withdrawn or cancelled and the session has not written to the note since, the hook logs `compact-pointer-stale-note` and tells the session to read the note for background only and not to claim it. The note predates the work done since the withdraw, so the session updates it at the next nudge. Pi does the same on `session_compact` and OpenCode on `session.compacted`: each resets the session's nudge level, drops a nudge it still holds, because that text quotes the fill the compaction freed, and sends the claim instructions for the open note, with Pi's sent as a hidden message and OpenCode's added to the next system prompt. Each logs `compact-pointer`. When the record for the note was withdrawn and the session has not written to the note since, Pi and OpenCode send the same read-only note text Claude does and log `compact-pointer-stale-note` instead. OpenCode keeps that text in the system prompt only while the withdrawal stands. Codex has no compaction hook here, so their sessions follow the claim command `keep` printed. Before it resets the nudge level or points at a note, the hook skips when the event carries no `transcript_path`, when the transcript file is not... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n267: \n268: ### Sub-Agents And Nested Runs\n269: \n270: An OpenCode `task` sub-agent is a child session with its own id. The plugin records every session whose `session.created` or `session.updated` event carries `info.parentID` and skips it everywhere, so a child never gets a nudge, a seed or an arm. The plugin tracks each child's `parentID` and a `busy` flag in `childSessions`, and a child stays `busy` from its creation until its `session.idle` or `session.deleted` event. A parent `session.idle` with a busy child is held in `deferredArms` and arms nothing, and the child that ends last runs the deferred arm for its parent. The plugin sets the session title from `SKILLED_LOOP_NAME` on a claim seed through `ctx.client.session.update`, and the call is not awaited, so an absent or failing `update` still leaves the seed in the system prompt. Pi has no built-in sub-agent.\n271: \n272: A worker an orchestrator dispatches through a CLI is marked with `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1`. On every adapter, the nudge decision returns `child-session` for it at any fill, so a worker never gets told to save continuity or write a handoff into its parent's packet. The claim path skips such a child the same way.\n273: \n274: A runtime started from inside a looped session, such as `claude -p` or `pi -p` run from a tool shell, inherits `SKILLED_LOOP_ID` without being the loop's runtime. The Claude, Pi and OpenCode adapters and the `prepare`, `keep` and `cancel` commands check the process tree first (the Codex adapters do not): when two or more processes sit between the host runtime and the loop pid, the run is nested. The host is the nearest ancestor that is neither a shell nor an OpenCode process whose parent is also an OpenCode process. OpenCode 2 runs each tool shell under a pty daemon that its serve process starts, and counting those two would read the loop's own OpenCode session as nested. A nested run logs `loop-nested-run` and drops every `SKILLED_LOOP_` variable from its own environment. That includes `SKILLED_LOOP_MANUAL_TEMPLATE` and the carried model, name and provider values, which would otherwise make the nested run print the outer loop's command. A nested run then behaves as a session outside the loop, and `prepare` there exits 3 instead of arming the outer loop. `cancel` drops the inherited loop id the same way, so it never cancels the outer hand-over unless you pass `--loop-id`. The check fails open, so a process table it cannot read leaves the variables in place.\n275: \n276: ---\n277: \n278: ## 4. STATE FILES\n279: \n280: The relaunch record uses `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/skilled-relaunch` when `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is set. When it is unset, the record uses `skilled-relaunch-<uid>` under the operating system temporary directory, because a shared temp directory would otherwise let the first user on a host create a directory the second user cannot own. The directory must be a real directory owned by the current user. A symlink, a plain file or a foreign owner fails with `relaunch state directory must be a real directory owned by the current user: <dir>`, and a directory that passes is set to mode 0700. Its filename is `relaunch-<loop-id>.json`.\n281: \n282: | Relaunch status | Meaning |\n283: |---|---|\n284: | pending | Prepare accepted the note. The runtime's arm event has not armed the record yet. |\n285: | armed | The runtime's arm event armed the record and started the killer: `Stop` on Claude and Codex, `agent_settled` on Pi, and `session.idle` on OpenCode. |\n286: | disarmed | The record ended without a relaunch. A session that never went idle before the deadline, a transcript unreadable for 1 s and a session still running after the SIGTERM grace period end with reason `killer-timeout`. A record whose hop count already reached `SESSION_HANDOVER_MAX_HOPS` ends with reason `hop-cap`, and one past its age limit ends with reason `stale`. In both cases the killer sends no signal. A new turn in the arming session ends an armed or deferred record with reason `user-message` on Pi and OpenCode, and so does an operator prompt over an armed or deferred record on Claude and Codex. `cancel` ends a pending or armed record with reason `cancelled`. `should-relaunch` reports both of those reasons as `withdrawn` and every other record that is not armed as `not-armed`. |\n287: \n288: A corrupt relaunch file is treated as absent. `should-relaunch` declines it with reason `no-file` and logs `corrupt-relaunch-file`. A record that parses but holds no `handoff` string is read the same way, because no writer produces one and a pending record without a note would let Stop arm a hop that has nothing to claim. A record whose `handoff` is an empty string is still read, and `should-relaunch` declines it as `incomplete-record`. The CLI also treats `EACCES`, `ENOTDIR` and `EISDIR` while reading the record as absent. Other errors return exit 2.\n289: \n290: The handoff note has a separate status field:\n291: \n292: | Note status | Meaning |\n293: |---|---|\n294: | open | Written and ready for a receiving session. |\n295: | claimed | The receiving session claimed it. The note records `claimed_by` and `claimed_at`. |\n296: | done | The receiving session completed the handoff, or `prepare` closed an earlier note of this session when it prepared a successor hop. |\n297: \n298: When a new hop goes ahead, `prepare` marks as `done` every other note this session claimed and every other open note this session wrote, adds `completed_at` and sets `superseded_by` to the new note's basename.\n299: \n300: The hook keeps per-session nudge levels in `nudge-<session-id>.json` in the same runtime state directory. After a withdraw or a `cancel` the file also holds the one extra nudge as a `rearm` field. The field starts pending and becomes a fill floor, the fill at the first check at or past the threshold plus 0.05, without nudging on that check. A delivered nudge or a compaction writes the level alone and so spends the grant. Every write to a relaunch record (prepare, arm, disarm, defer, withdraw and the removal `consume` makes) and the claim and completion of a note run under a lock beside the file, so two writers cannot both act on the same read. A lock that cannot be taken lets the write go ahead without it. The hooks and wrapper append events to `relaunch.log` on a best-effort basis. The killer appends its events there too. A failed append in the killer is not swallowed. Each event is one line, appended under fsync at mode 0600, with the mode re-pinned on every append. When the previous last line has no newline, as after a partial write, the new event starts on its own line, so concurrent writers keep one event per line. A short write, from a full disk or a file-size limit, throws `relaunch log short write: wrote X of Y bytes to <path>` instead of losing the event quietly. The killer lock file stores the owner pid, the creation timestamp and the expiry stamp. A lock past its expiry counts as stale, so a reused pid cannot keep the loop pinned. Active or newly created locks log `killer-lock-held`. Stale locks log `killer-lock-stale` before the killer removes and retries them.\n301: \n302: ### Hop History\n303: \n304: `relaunch.log` sits in a temporary directory the system clears, so it is no record of past hops. Each hop therefore also appends one line to `.skilled/skills/.state/session-handover/hop-history.log`, beside the handoff notes. That folder is gitignored and persists. A repository that links this checkout's `.skilled` writes to the same file, and each line carries the session's folder so you can tell them apart.\n305: \n306: | Event | Written when |\n307: |---|---|\n308: | `handed-over` | The wrapper passed its last checks and relaunched, so the session handed over. `should-relaunch` writes nothing, because the wrapper can still decline after it approves. The line carries the hop number. |\n309: | `claimed` | A session claimed the note. The line ends with the first eight characters of the claiming session's id, and `history` prints them as `by <id>`. The note's file name names the session that wrote it, so a session that claimed its own note shows the same id in both. |\n310: | `done` | The fresh session completed the note. |\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 311]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 120, \"offset\": 191, \"path\": \".skilled/hooks/session-handover/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794808058788,"end":1794808059284}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794808059289,"sessionID":"ses_e1d1c612bffenqRaMYnBfMfTGv","part":{"partID":"prt_1e2e5946d0013nVIz1089qxM2o","sessionID":"ses_e1d1c612bffenqRaMYnBfMfTGv","messageID":"msg_1e2e5823e0017lI4GLnwKuH4vi","type":"tool","id":"call_01_4d9pjj2354oy6esuae575ay4","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":130,"offset":1040,"path":".skilled/hooks/session-handover/lib/relaunch-core.cjs"},"output":"Read file .skilled/hooks/session-handover/lib/relaunch-core.cjs, lines 1040-1169\n1040: \n1041: // Vibe records the session's folder in `meta.json`, not in the journal the reader parses, so\n1042: // the mint reads the metadata file beside that journal instead of looking for a cwd in it. A\n1043: // meta that names another session is the one the reader refuses at redeem, so it refuses too.\n1044: function vibeWorkingFolder(sessionFolder, sessionId) {\n1045:   try {\n1046:     const meta = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(sessionFolder, 'meta.json'), 'utf8'));\n1047:     if (nonEmptyString(meta?.session_id) && meta.session_id !== sessionId) return null;\n1048:     if (nonEmptyString(meta?.environment?.working_directory)) {\n1049:       return meta.environment.working_directory;\n1050:     }\n1051:     return nonEmptyString(meta?.origin_directory) ? meta.origin_directory : null;\n1052:   } catch {\n1053:     return null;\n1054:   }\n1055: }\n1056: \n1057: /**\n1058:  * Build the history lookup section a claim prints for the session that wrote the note.\n1059:  *\n1060:  * The locator is minted at claim time, because the lookup CLI refuses a locator older than\n1061:  * half an hour: a fresh mint is what lets a note claimed hours after it was prepared still\n1062:  * redeem. The printed command moves to the\n1063:  * locator's workspace, so the section is withheld unless that workspace is the receiver's\n1064:  * own checkout, which is the only place the command can run.\n1065:  *\n1066:  * @param {Object} options - The claimed note and the receiver.\n1067:  * @param {Object<string, string>} options.frontmatter - The note's parsed frontmatter.\n1068:  * @param {string} [options.cwd=process.cwd()] - Folder the claiming session runs in.\n1069:  * @param {Date|number|string} [options.now] - Clock value for a deterministic locator.\n1070:  * @returns {string|null} The markdown section, or null when no lookup could run.\n1071:  */\n1072: function handoverLookupSection({ frontmatter, cwd = process.cwd(), now } = {}) {\n1073:   try {\n1074:     const sessionId = frontmatter?.session_id;\n1075:     const runtime = frontmatter?.runtime;\n1076:     const noteCwd = frontmatter?.cwd;\n1077:     if (\n1078:       !nonEmptyString(sessionId)\n1079:       || !nonEmptyString(runtime)\n1080:       || !nonEmptyString(noteCwd)\n1081:     ) {\n1082:       return null;\n1083:     }\n1084:     // The mint writes the sender's hook state, so the checkouts are compared before it runs\n1085:     // and a note from another checkout leaves that state untouched.\n1086:     const offer = loadLookupOffer();\n1087:     const receiverPlacement = offer.placeLookup(cwd);\n1088:     const senderPlacement = offer.placeLookup(noteCwd);\n1089:     if (\n1090:       receiverPlacement === null\n1091:       || senderPlacement === null\n1092:       || !sameCheckout(receiverPlacement.workspace, senderPlacement.workspace)\n1093:     ) {\n1094:       return null;\n1095:     }\n1096:     const locator = mintHandoverLocator({ runtime, sessionId, cwd: noteCwd, now });\n1097:     if (locator === null) return null;\n1098:     return offer.buildHistoryLookupSection(locator, true);\n1099:   } catch {\n1100:     return null;\n1101:   }\n1102: }\n1103: \n1104: /**\n1105:  * Find the model and effort the sending session is using now, so the relaunched session can\n1106:  * start on them. Claude is read from the last main-chain assistant entry, Codex from the last\n1107:  * turn context, and Cursor from a CLI config change made during this hop, whose effort rides\n1108:  * inside the model. Pi hands its values over when it arms. Every reader fails\n1109:  * closed, since a hand-over never fails for want of a setting, and an empty string means the\n1110:  * value is not proven.\n1111:  *\n1112:  * @param {Object} options - The session to read.\n1113:  * @param {string} options.runtime - Runtime that owns the session.\n1114:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - The session's identifier.\n1115:  * @param {NodeJS.ProcessEnv} [options.env=process.env] - Environment carrying the log location.\n1116:  * @returns {{model: string, effort: string, name: string, generatedName: string}} The values\n1117:  * the log proves, `''` for the rest, the live session name when the log carries one, and the\n1118:  * title the runtime generated when the session has no other name.\n1119:  */\n1120: function resolveSessionSettings({ runtime, sessionId, env = process.env } = {}) {\n1121:   const none = { model: '', effort: '', name: '', generatedName: '' };\n1122:   try {\n1123:     if (!nonEmptyString(sessionId)) return none;\n1124:     const usage = require('./context-usage.cjs');\n1125:     let found = null;\n1126:     if (runtime === 'claude') {\n1127:       const logPath = claudeSessionLogPath(sessionId, env);\n1128:       found = logPath === null ? null : usage.readClaudeSettings(logPath);\n1129:     } else if (runtime === 'codex') {\n1130:       const rolloutPath = codexRolloutPath(sessionId, env);\n1131:       found = rolloutPath === null ? null : usage.readCodexSettings(rolloutPath);\n1132:     } else if (runtime === 'cursor') {\n1133:       found = { model: cursorSelectedModel(env), effort: '' };\n1134:     }\n1135:     if (found === null) return none;\n1136:     return {\n1137:       model: found.model ?? '',\n1138:       effort: found.effort ?? '',\n1139:       name: typeof found.name === 'string' ? found.name : '',\n1140:       generatedName: typeof found.generatedName === 'string' ? found.generatedName : '',\n1141:     };\n1142:   } catch {\n1143:     return none;\n1144:   }\n1145: }\n1146: \n1147: function recordedGoalPacket({ runtime, sessionId, roots }) {\n1148:   for (const root of roots) {\n1149:     let record = null;\n1150:     try {\n1151:       record = goalRecordFor(runtime, sessionId, root);\n1152:     } catch {\n1153:       // A record that cannot be read is a goal that cannot be proven.\n1154:     }\n1155:     const packet = record?.status === 'active' && typeof record.packetPath === 'string'\n1156:       ? record.packetPath\n1157:       : '';\n1158:     if (packet !== '' && goalPacketProblem(packet, roots) === null) return packet;\n1159:   }\n1160:   return '';\n1161: }\n1162: \n1163: /**\n1164:  * Walk each checkout's specs tree for the goal files that carry the headline. A matched packet\n1165:  * is proven the same way a recorded one is, and the caller decides whether one match is unique\n1166:  * enough to carry.\n1167:  *\n1168:  * @param {Object} options - The search.\n1169:  * @param {string[]} options.roots - Absolute checkouts to walk.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1170]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/hooks/session-handover/lib/relaunch-core.cjs, lines 1040-1169\n1040: \n1041: // Vibe records the session's folder in `meta.json`, not in the journal the reader parses, so\n1042: // the mint reads the metadata file beside that journal instead of looking for a cwd in it. A\n1043: // meta that names another session is the one the reader refuses at redeem, so it refuses too.\n1044: function vibeWorkingFolder(sessionFolder, sessionId) {\n1045:   try {\n1046:     const meta = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(path.join(sessionFolder, 'meta.json'), 'utf8'));\n1047:     if (nonEmptyString(meta?.session_id) && meta.session_id !== sessionId) return null;\n1048:     if (nonEmptyString(meta?.environment?.working_directory)) {\n1049:       return meta.environment.working_directory;\n1050:     }\n1051:     return nonEmptyString(meta?.origin_directory) ? meta.origin_directory : null;\n1052:   } catch {\n1053:     return null;\n1054:   }\n1055: }\n1056: \n1057: /**\n1058:  * Build the history lookup section a claim prints for the session that wrote the note.\n1059:  *\n1060:  * The locator is minted at claim time, because the lookup CLI refuses a locator older than\n1061:  * half an hour: a fresh mint is what lets a note claimed hours after it was prepared still\n1062:  * redeem. The printed command moves to the\n1063:  * locator's workspace, so the section is withheld unless that workspace is the receiver's\n1064:  * own checkout, which is the only place the command can run.\n1065:  *\n1066:  * @param {Object} options - The claimed note and the receiver.\n1067:  * @param {Object<string, string>} options.frontmatter - The note's parsed frontmatter.\n1068:  * @param {string} [options.cwd=process.cwd()] - Folder the claiming session runs in.\n1069:  * @param {Date|number|string} [options.now] - Clock value for a deterministic locator.\n1070:  * @returns {string|null} The markdown section, or null when no lookup could run.\n1071:  */\n1072: function handoverLookupSection({ frontmatter, cwd = process.cwd(), now } = {}) {\n1073:   try {\n1074:     const sessionId = frontmatter?.session_id;\n1075:     const runtime = frontmatter?.runtime;\n1076:     const noteCwd = frontmatter?.cwd;\n1077:     if (\n1078:       !nonEmptyString(sessionId)\n1079:       || !nonEmptyString(runtime)\n1080:       || !nonEmptyString(noteCwd)\n1081:     ) {\n1082:       return null;\n1083:     }\n1084:     // The mint writes the sender's hook state, so the checkouts are compared before it runs\n1085:     // and a note from another checkout leaves that state untouched.\n1086:     const offer = loadLookupOffer();\n1087:     const receiverPlacement = offer.placeLookup(cwd);\n1088:     const senderPlacement = offer.placeLookup(noteCwd);\n1089:     if (\n1090:       receiverPlacement === null\n1091:       || senderPlacement === null\n1092:       || !sameCheckout(receiverPlacement.workspace, senderPlacement.workspace)\n1093:     ) {\n1094:       return null;\n1095:     }\n1096:     const locator = mintHandoverLocator({ runtime, sessionId, cwd: noteCwd, now });\n1097:     if (locator === null) return null;\n1098:     return offer.buildHistoryLookupSection(locator, true);\n1099:   } catch {\n1100:     return null;\n1101:   }\n1102: }\n1103: \n1104: /**\n1105:  * Find the model and effort the sending session is using now, so the relaunched session can\n1106:  * start on them. Claude is read from the last main-chain assistant entry, Codex from the last\n1107:  * turn context, and Cursor from a CLI config change made during this hop, whose effort rides\n1108:  * inside the model. Pi hands its values over when it arms. Every reader fails\n1109:  * closed, since a hand-over never fails for want of a setting, and an empty string means the\n1110:  * value is not proven.\n1111:  *\n1112:  * @param {Object} options - The session to read.\n1113:  * @param {string} options.runtime - Runtime that owns the session.\n1114:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - The session's identifier.\n1115:  * @param {NodeJS.ProcessEnv} [options.env=process.env] - Environment carrying the log location.\n1116:  * @returns {{model: string, effort: string, name: string, generatedName: string}} The values\n1117:  * the log proves, `''` for the rest, the live session name when the log carries one, and the\n1118:  * title the runtime generated when the session has no other name.\n1119:  */\n1120: function resolveSessionSettings({ runtime, sessionId, env = process.env } = {}) {\n1121:   const none = { model: '', effort: '', name: '', generatedName: '' };\n1122:   try {\n1123:     if (!nonEmptyString(sessionId)) return none;\n1124:     const usage = require('./context-usage.cjs');\n1125:     let found = null;\n1126:     if (runtime === 'claude') {\n1127:       const logPath = claudeSessionLogPath(sessionId, env);\n1128:       found = logPath === null ? null : usage.readClaudeSettings(logPath);\n1129:     } else if (runtime === 'codex') {\n1130:       const rolloutPath = codexRolloutPath(sessionId, env);\n1131:       found = rolloutPath === null ? null : usage.readCodexSettings(rolloutPath);\n1132:     } else if (runtime === 'cursor') {\n1133:       found = { model: cursorSelectedModel(env), effort: '' };\n1134:     }\n1135:     if (found === null) return none;\n1136:     return {\n1137:       model: found.model ?? '',\n1138:       effort: found.effort ?? '',\n1139:       name: typeof found.name === 'string' ? found.name : '',\n1140:       generatedName: typeof found.generatedName === 'string' ? found.generatedName : '',\n1141:     };\n1142:   } catch {\n1143:     return none;\n1144:   }\n1145: }\n1146: \n1147: function recordedGoalPacket({ runtime, sessionId, roots }) {\n1148:   for (const root of roots) {\n1149:     let record = null;\n1150:     try {\n1151:       record = goalRecordFor(runtime, sessionId, root);\n1152:     } catch {\n1153:       // A record that cannot be read is a goal that cannot be proven.\n1154:     }\n1155:     const packet = record?.status === 'active' && typeof record.packetPath === 'string'\n1156:       ? record.packetPath\n1157:       : '';\n1158:     if (packet !== '' && goalPacketProblem(packet, roots) === null) return packet;\n1159:   }\n1160:   return '';\n1161: }\n1162: \n1163: /**\n1164:  * Walk each checkout's specs tree for the goal files that carry the headline. A matched packet\n1165:  * is proven the same way a recorded one is, and the caller decides whether one match is unique\n1166:  * enough to carry.\n1167:  *\n1168:  * @param {Object} options - The search.\n1169:  * @param {string[]} options.roots - Absolute checkouts to walk.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1170]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 130, \"offset\": 1040, \"path\": \".skilled/hooks/session-handover/lib/relaunch-core.cjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794808058989,"end":1794808059289}}}}
